window.SFW_SEARCH = [{"t":"Documentation","u":"docs/index.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Start here","start-here"],["How StockFW is put together","how-stockfw-is-put-together"],["How to read these pages","how-to-read-these-pages"],["Version","version"],["The longer document","the-longer-document"]],"x":"StockFW documentation StockFW is a desktop firewall manager for Windows and Linux. It does not filter packets itself. It configures the firewall engine your operating system already has, shows you every connection that engine allowed or blocked, keeps your policy as plain text, and lets you try a change before you make it. Start here# Install on Windows: the installer, the elevated shortcut, and enabling monitoring. Install on Linux: the tarball, sudo and its search path, and the command line. Your first ten minutes: check protection, watch events, add a rule, apply it, ask the simulator a question. How StockFW is put together# Four parts, and knowing them makes every page of the app predictable. The engine Your operating system’s firewall: the Windows Filtering Platform behind Windows Defender Firewall, or netfilter behind iptables on Linux. StockFW adds and removes rules in it through supported tools and never replaces it. If StockFW is closed or crashes, the engine keeps enforcing whatever was last applied. The policy What you want the engine to do, stored as one small comma-separated file per rule kind in your profile. The Policy page, the Copilot and the command line all edit the same files. Saving a rule does not change the firewall; Apply does. The events What the engine decided, read from the log it already writes: Security events 5156 and 5157 on Windows, the kernel ring buffer on Linux. StockFW never sees packets, only verdicts, and it shows them as they are. The gate Every path that could change the firewall, the Apply button, an event’s right-click menu, a Copilot suggestion, Sentinel’s auto-block, stockfw apply, goes through one function. It measures the change against what is live, records what it applied, rolls back if the engine refuses, and writes one audit line. There is no second door. How to read these pages# Claims about behaviour carry a stamp. A stamp is a promise about evidence, not decoration: Verified test name An automated test in the repository proves it. The name is the test, so you can run it: most live in port/tests/ and in tests/test-core.sh. Measured a number A number from an experiment that was run, such as the 150-second memory soak. Known gap A limit StockFW has, written down so it is not mistaken for a feature. Where the two platforms differ, a badge says which one a sentence is about: Windows Linux. Everything without a badge applies to both. Version# These pages describe StockFW 1.0.8, the release on the download page. What changed in earlier versions is in the changelog. The longer document# These pages explain how to use StockFW. The reasoning behind it, the threat model with file-and-line evidence, every defect found and how it was fixed, and the measurements, are in the architecture and security document in the repository, one chapter per release. When these pages and that document disagree, the document is right and this site has a bug; please report it."},{"t":"Activity","u":"docs/activity.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["The controls","the-controls"],["Policy activity","policy-activity"],["Network I/O","network-i-o"],["Why the grey band","why-the-grey-band"],["Reading a point","reading-a-point"],["How the data is kept","how-the-data-is-kept"]],"x":"Activity Two graphs over the last 15 minutes to 24 hours: how many connections your policy allowed and blocked per minute, and how many bytes per second crossed your adapters. Fifteen minutes on the staged network: scans and connections for a few minutes, then quiet. Each point is one 10-second slot; the tiles count the whole hour. The controls# Range: 15 min, 1 hour, 6 hours, 24 hours. Both graphs follow it. Adapter: all adapters together, or one. It affects the Network I/O graph and the throughput tile. Show as table: every interval as a row, for reading exact numbers or copying them. The three tiles summarise the range: blocked and allowed counts for the last hour and the last 24 hours, with “counting since 16:23” when the app has been open for less than that, and the current throughput in and out. Policy activity# Events per minute, allowed in blue and blocked in red. It counts exactly what the Events page lists, after that page’s filters and its duplicate check, and it counts each event at the event’s own time, not when the app happened to read it. That matters on Windows, where the first poll delivers a batch of recent history: those events land in their own minutes, and anything older than the app is dropped into the no-data band rather than piled into the current minute as a false spike. Verified fs-activity-smoke On Linux the allowed line is only as real as the kernel log: the policy logs the first packet of each new allowed connection when Log allowed connections is on. With it off, the card says the line is not drawn, rather than drawing a flat line that would claim nothing was allowed. Network I/O# Bytes per second received and sent, from the adapters’ own cumulative counters: GetIfTable2 on Windows, /proc/net/dev on Linux. No packet capture, no trace session, no elevation. A counter that goes backwards (a driver reload) starts a new baseline rather than drawing negative traffic. Verified test-core.sh Why the grey band# A graph that starts at zero looks like a quiet night. StockFW did not observe anything before it was opened, so that stretch is shaded “no data” and never drawn as a line. The newest point is partial, and its rate divides by the seconds that have actually elapsed, so the last sample is not drawn artificially low. Verified fs-activity-smoke Reading a point# Move the mouse over a graph for a crosshair and a tooltip with the values at that interval; or focus the graph and use the arrow keys. Each graph has one y axis with round gridlines, time ticks spaced for their labels, a legend and a label at the end of each line, and the colours were checked for contrast in light mode: allowed and received #2a78d6, blocked #e34948, sent #eb6834. How the data is kept# Counters are kept in 10-second slots, 8,640 per series (24 hours), at most 24 series, about 1.6 MB when full, so a week in the tray costs the same memory as an hour. Nothing is written to disk; the graphs are about this session. Verified test-core.sh The dashboard draws the same two graphs from the same code, and can backfill from the kernel log so its history starts before it was opened."},{"t":"Background, tray and sign-in","u":"docs/background.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Turning it on","turning-it-on"],["Starting hidden","starting-hidden"],["One copy per user","one-copy-per-user"],["The icon and its tooltip","the-icon-and-its-tooltip"],["The menu","the-menu"],["Start at sign-in","sign-in"],["At boot, before anyone signs in","at-boot-before-anyone-signs-in"],["What runs in the background","what-runs-in-the-background"],["Where the icon cannot appear","where-the-icon-cannot-appear"]],"x":"Background, tray and sign-in The firewall engine runs whether StockFW is open or not; StockFW in the background is what keeps watching events, learning, and, in auto mode, letting Sentinel act. The tray icon is how you know it is there and how you get it back. Turning it on# Two settings on Preferences › General, both on by default: Show a tray icon and Close and minimize to the tray. With both on, closing or minimising the window hides it and StockFW keeps running. With the icon off, closing the window quits the program; StockFW will not hide a window it cannot give back, because a background process with no way to reopen it would be a zombie with root rights. Verified ui-tray-smoke Starting hidden# StockFW --background (or --minimized) starts StockFW in the notification area with the window hidden. The Events page and everything behind it are built whether or not the window is shown, so a hidden copy lists and saves events exactly as a visible one does. If no notification area takes the icon, the window is shown instead, so nothing runs invisibly. Verified test-instance.sh One copy per user# Starting StockFW again does not start a second one: the launch hands over to the running copy and brings its window forward, then exits. StockFW --quit asks the running copy to quit cleanly, saving its settings on the way. A launch with more rights takes over from a copy with fewer: sudo StockFW replaces your unprivileged copy, and on Windows StockFW (Elevated) replaces the ordinary one, after the old copy has closed its files. Verified test-instance.sh The icon and its tooltip# The tooltip reads StockFW, Protected or StockFW, Not protected, with the engine’s name, from the same state the Overview shows; when the state cannot be read it says Firewall state is unknown rather than guessing. The icon follows the state too, so the answer to “is my firewall running?” is one glance. The menu# A status header: the firewall state and the last few blocks, live. Open StockFW brings the window back. Pause protection and Pause Sentinel, and Start firewall or Stop firewall, so the common actions never need the window. What the running copy cannot do, because it has no root or administrator rights, is greyed with the reason rather than offered and failed. Start at sign-in, a switch; see below. Preferences, Help (opens the Help window at the “Running in the background” topic, leaving the main window hidden) and About StockFW. Quit StockFW ends the program. It does not stop the firewall: the engine keeps enforcing whatever was last applied, exactly as it would if StockFW crashed. To stop filtering, use Stop firewall here or in the window, or run sudo stockfw stop. Start at sign-in# From the tray menu, from Preferences › General, or with StockFW --enable-autostart and --disable-autostart. On Windows it is a Task Scheduler task that runs without administrator rights, so no UAC prompt appears at sign-in; on Linux it is an XDG autostart entry in ~/.config/autostart. Both start the unprivileged copy in the background: it monitors and learns, shows the firewall state as it can read it, and the elevated shortcut or sudo is still what changes the firewall. The uninstaller removes your entry. Verified test-autostart.sh At boot, before anyone signs in# Windows Windows Firewall rules persist, so StockFW’s rules are in force from boot. Linux iptables rules are gone at boot, so the Linux installer enables stockfw.service, which loads the last policy applied as root before the network comes up. The service and StockFW in the tray are separate things: one puts the rules back, the other watches them. At boot has the details. Verified test-boot-start.sh What runs in the background# Event monitoring, and everything fed by it: the Activity counters, the Copilot’s checks and what it learns, Sentinel’s detectors, and the event history if you keep one. Sentinel’s ten-minute expiry timer for auto-blocks. The hourly check for threat-intel feed updates, for the feeds you have turned on. Saving: settings and what the Copilot has learned are written every five minutes when they changed, and when the session ends, so a crash loses at most five minutes. Memory stays flat: the Activity series are fixed-size rings, the Events list is a fixed ring of the size you chose (about 7 MB at the default 100,000), the Copilot keeps the last 100 changes, and a 150-second soak at full event rate found no growth. Measured 150-second soak, docs chapter 9 Where the icon cannot appear# A tray needs a tray host and an X11 display. GNOME has not shipped a host since 3.26; the AppIndicator extension restores it, and KDE, XFCE, MATE, Cinnamon and Windows have one built in. Without a host the icon simply does not show, the window keeps working (a hidden start shows it), and closing it quits, so nothing is lost. The project’s own tray test fails on a headless machine for this reason, and is expected to. Known gap Linux GTK has a tray only on X11 and Windows. On a Wayland session (GNOME and KDE Plasma by default, and WSL’s WSLg) StockFW does not try to make one: a background start shows the window, closing the window quits, and Preferences greys both tray switches with the reason. The once-a-minute re-read of the firewall’s state, which is how stockfw stop in a terminal reaches the Overview and the sidebar, runs whenever the window is open, with or without a tray icon. Under sudo StockFW runs on XWayland, because sudo drops the Wayland display variables, so the tray works there as on X11. Verified test-notray.sh"},{"t":"Command line","u":"docs/cli.html","s":"Command line","h":[["Before you start: whose policy?","before-you-start-whose-policy"],["How options are read","options"],["status","status"],["policy list, show, add, remove, diff","policy"],["check: lines that are not rules","check"],["apply, start, stop","apply"],["The lockout guard over SSH","lockout"],["simulate","simulate"],["events","events"],["Saved history","events-history"],["Triage with --summary","events-summary"],["The live event line: events -f","events-follow"],["audit","audit"],["sentinel list","sentinel"],["copilot health","copilot-health"],["listening: open ports and who can reach them","listening"],["connections, interfaces","connections"],["dashboard","dashboard"],["The manual page and completion","man"],["A build of the command line alone","no-gui"],["Output conventions","output-conventions"],["Environment variables","environment-variables"],["intel","intel"],["The app’s own flags","app-flags"],["Tests","tests"]],"x":"Command line stockfw is the desktop app without the display: the same policy files, the same simulator, the same apply gate. A rule added here is the rule the Policy page shows, and an apply here writes the same audit line. It is the tool for a server, an SSH session or WSL, where there is no window to open. Linux There is no Windows command line; on Windows, use the app. stockfw --helpCopyUsage: stockfw [--json] [--plain] [--color=WHEN] [-q] <command> [args] StockFW from the command line: the same policy, simulator and apply gate as the desktop app, without a display. Commands: status Firewall state, policy and monitoring policy list|show|add|... Read, edit and diff the traffic policy check Lines of the policy files that are not rules apply [--dry-run] Push the saved policy to the firewall (root) start | stop Start or stop the firewall (root) simulate \"<traffic>\" What-if: would this traffic be allowed? copilot health Check the policy for overlaps and risks events [-n N] [-f] Recent firewall events; --history for saved connections Active TCP connections and their programs listening Open ports, their programs and who can reach them interfaces [--watch] Network adapters with traffic and rates audit [-n N] [-f] The audit log: every apply, start and stop sentinel [list] Sentinel's staged blocks (read-only) intel <action> Threat-intel blocklists (stockfw help intel) dashboard [--port N] Serve the local web dashboard and open it help [command] Help for one command version Print the version Global options: --json Machine-readable output (one JSON document; NDJSON for -f) --plain, -H Tables as tab-separated rows, no header, no colour --full, -l Never cut a table cell to fit the terminal --color=WHEN auto (the default), always or never; --no-color is never (NO_COLOR and TERM=dumb are honoured too) -q, --quiet Only errors and the result: no hints or notes -h, --help This help; -V, --version the version -- The end of the options: what follows is plain words Exit status: 0 success 2 usage error 5 simulate: BLOCKED; 1 failure 3 needs root privileges intel lookup: blocked 6 simulate: DEPENDS 7 copilot health: findings Policy files live in ~/.stockfw (or /etc/stockfw when run as root) -- the same files the desktop app edits. Nothing here talks to the network, except `stockfw intel update' downloading the blocklists and the location database you turned on; the dashboard listens on 127.0.0.1 only. Before you start: whose policy?# As your own user the policy lives in ~/.stockfw; as root, in /etc/stockfw. A rule added without sudo is not the rule sudo stockfw apply pushes. Pick one identity and keep to it; the simplest is sudo for everything, so the app, the command line and the firewall all read /etc/stockfw. For a script that must not depend on who runs it, set STOCKFW_RULES_DIR. On Fedora, RHEL and some Kali systems sudo does not search /usr/local/bin (Ubuntu’s default does), so write sudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw there, or add an alias; a hint that needs root always prints the full path, so you can paste it. See Install on Linux. How options are read# The word after an option that takes a value is that value: --comment -h saves the comment -h. Everything after -- is a plain word, so a host or comment that starts with a dash goes after it, or as --comment=TEXT. An option a command does not know is refused with exit 2, never ignored and never folded into a simulate question. A mistyped command or option gets a suggestion: TerminalCopy$ stockfw polcy list stockfw: error: unknown command: polcy (did you mean `stockfw policy'?) stockfw: hint: run `stockfw help' for the list of commands $ stockfw simulate \"inbound rdp\" --bogus stockfw: error: unknown option for simulate: --bogus stockfw: hint: see `stockfw help simulate'; a word that starts with a dash goes after -- $ echo $? 2 Tables are fitted to the terminal, and only to the terminal: a pipe or a file gets every character, --full never cuts a cell, and --plain prints tab-separated rows with no header for cut and awk. Text that came from a log or a comment is escaped before it reaches your terminal: control characters and bidi controls print as \\xNN or \\uNNNN, in every table, in events --csv, in the rule names of simulate and in the list names of intel. Symbols fall back to ASCII outside a UTF-8 locale. Verified test-cli.sh status# TerminalCopy$ sudo stockfw status StockFW 1.0.8 Linux (iptables) - web1 Firewall ● running IPv6 filtered Policy 9 rules, live Last apply 2026-11-03 18:11 policy applied (cli) Blocked today 45 - last TCP 23 to 203.0.113.9 at 18:04 Threat intel off (No blocklist is turned on.) Apply mode live Monitoring reading /dev/kmsg At boot stockfw.service enabled: the rules are put back at boot Adapters eth0 192.168.1.20 ↓ 84.5 MB ↑ 270.4 MB Connections 16 active $ stockfw status -q running, live The Policy line says whether what you saved is what is live: “9 rules, live”, “1 change not applied (stockfw policy diff)”, “never applied”, or “settings changed since the last apply” when the Firewall preferences you saved are not the ones that went out. Last apply is the newest apply, start or stop in the audit log, with cli or gui. Blocked counts since local midnight, from the saved event history while it is kept and otherwise from the kernel log. Monitoring is decided by what your account can actually read, tried rather than guessed from your user id: a user in the adm group sees “reading /var/log/kern.log”. At boot says whether stockfw.service is enabled, installed but off, or absent. Reading the firewall state needs root; without it the state is unknown, not guessed. -v adds the rules directory, the firewall settings (outbound mode, DROP or REJECT, ICMP, logging, interfaces, NAT), the event history and Sentinel. With --json the 1.0.7 keys are unchanged and new ones are added: live (live, pending, never…) with pending counts, last_apply, blocked_today with last_block, monitoring with its source, boot_unit and host. Verified test-cli.sh policy list, show, add, remove, diff# TerminalCopy$ sudo stockfw policy add inbound-allow-service SSH 22 192.168.1.0/24 --comment \"admin LAN\" Added inbound-allow-service:1 SSH port 22 for 192.168.1.0/24 [IPv4 only] Check: inbound tcp port 22 from 192.168.1.1 -> ALLOWED (saved policy) Not live yet: /usr/local/bin/stockfw apply (preview: stockfw policy diff) $ sudo stockfw policy add outbound-deny-to 198.51.100.66 --comment \"known bad host\" Added outbound-deny-to:1 198.51.100.66 [IPv4 only] Check: outbound tcp to 198.51.100.66 -> BLOCKED (saved policy) Not live yet: /usr/local/bin/stockfw apply (preview: stockfw policy diff) $ sudo stockfw policy list --status inbound-allow-service (2) inbound-allow-service:1 SSH port 22 for 192.168.1.0/24 [IPv4 only] pending # admin LAN inbound-allow-service:2 HTTPS port 443 for Anyone [IPv4 + IPv6] live # web server outbound-deny-to (1) outbound-deny-to:1 198.51.100.66 [IPv4 only] pending # known bad host outbound-deny-service (1) outbound-deny-service:1 Telnet port 23 for Anyone [IPv4 + IPv6] live # no cleartext logins $ stockfw policy show inbound-allow-service:2 inbound-allow-service:2 Allow service HTTPS Port 443 For Anyone Comment web server Covers IPv4 + IPv6 Anyone: the rule applies to IPv4 and IPv6. Status live $ sudo stockfw policy diff Saved /etc/stockfw vs applied 2026-11-03 18:11:57 UTC + inbound-allow-service:1 SSH port 22 for 192.168.1.0/24 [IPv4 only] + outbound-deny-to:1 198.51.100.66 [IPv4 only] = 2 rules unchanged Firewall: running. Make it live: /usr/local/bin/stockfw apply $ sudo stockfw policy remove outbound-deny-to:1 Removed outbound-deny-to:1 198.51.100.66 [IPv4 only] Check: outbound tcp to 198.51.100.66 -> ALLOWED (saved policy) Not live yet: /usr/local/bin/stockfw apply (preview: stockfw policy diff) Every rule has a reference, KIND:N, numbered as policy list shows it; KIND#N and KIND N work too. show prints one rule with what it covers and whether it is live. add and remove echo the reference and run a What-If check"},{"t":"Copilot","u":"docs/copilot.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["What is on the page","what-is-on-the-page"],["The evidence drawer","the-evidence-drawer"],["The checks","the-checks"],["Policy health","policy-health"],["Sentinel: the one automatic action","sentinel-the-one-automatic-action"],["The audit trail","the-audit-trail"],["Honest limits","honest-limits"]],"x":"Copilot A set of fixed rules that run on this computer over the event log and the saved policy. It is not an AI model and has no network, no account and no prompt: it works on an air-gapped machine, sends nothing anywhere and cannot be talked into anything. Every finding names the rule that raised it, the numbers it measured next to the limits, and the events behind it. After a port scan and two rounds of login attempts on the staged network. Each row says what was seen, what What-If says about it, and when. What is on the page# Sentinel: Detect only or Detect + auto-block, with one sentence on what auto-block can do. Auto-block only stages outbound blocks to unknown public addresses, for ten minutes; everything else waits for you. Four tiles: Needs attention counts the Critical and Warning findings; Policy health counts the health check's findings, leaving out the expected “no effect in this mode” notes; Auto-blocks active; and Apps learned, or Services learned on Linux, where the log names no program. Findings: each row has a severity pill, a title, one sentence on what was seen, and the check and time. Click a row, or press Enter on it, to open its evidence drawer. Policy health: overlaps, conflicts and open risky services in the saved policy, with Check again, Show rule and, where one is safe, Fix. What-If simulator: a question about a connection in plain words. It has its own page. Changes made by the Copilot: every block it staged and every fix it made, newest first, each with Undo. An undone change, and a block that lifted, stay in the list marked with how and when they ended; the last 100 are kept. The list follows the events once a second and is updated in place: a row is rebuilt only when what it shows changed, and the keyboard focus stays on the same control. When there is nothing to show, the page says why: no events yet, monitoring off with the reason the Events page gives, or “No findings from N events… This is not a guarantee that nothing is wrong”, with the list of what is checked. Verified fs-copilot-smoke The evidence drawer# What we saw: up to twelve of the events behind the finding, each with its own time and verdict. Why flagged puts the measured numbers next to the limits. The health note under it explains itself and offers no fix, because the rows are right as they are. Every drawer has the same parts: What we saw, the events behind the finding; In full, the whole story in prose; Why flagged, the measured numbers next to the limits; Could be harmless, the innocent explanation; and Auto-block, what Sentinel did or why it did not. The text can be selected and copied, and a click inside the drawer does not close it. The actions: Block outbound to this address Offered only when What-If says the finding's connection is ALLOWED now and BLOCKED with the row, checked again just before writing, and confirmed in a dialog that prints the literal row. The row is saved with rollback on failure, pushed through the apply gate, audited, and listed under Changes with Undo. Ask What-If Puts the finding's connection into the What-If card, so you can see what the policy would do with it. A finding about an address a blocklist or a country rule already blocks has no such button, because What-If does not consult the lists and the address is blocked already. Show in Events Opens the Events page filtered to the finding's events. Dismiss Takes the finding off the list for this session. Dismissals are not saved. The checks# Counting is per program on Windows, where the Security log names the program. On Linux the kernel log names no program, so the key is the port's service name, https or ssh, and several programs on one port share one history. These checks run on every event and need no history: CheckTriggerSeverity Port scanOne source reaching 12 distinct local ports in a minute, or 25 in ten minutes. The addresses of one IPv6 /64 are also counted together. One new report per source per hour; later probes update the open finding.Info when every probe was refused, Warning when any reached an allowed port Login attemptsOne source, 10 inbound attempts in five minutes across SSH, RDP, VNC, Telnet, FTP and SMB, on their usual ports and on the ports your inbound rules give those services.Critical when the policy lets that source in on one of those ports and some attempts were refused; Warning or Info otherwise, and the sentence says which case applies Timed contacts (beacon)Outbound only. Six or more contacts with one destination, port and protocol at a mean interval of 10 seconds to 10 minutes and a spread of at most 30 percent of the mean. Time sync and DNS to this computer's own resolvers are never timed.Warning; Critical when a blocklist or country rule also matched it Fan-outOutbound, per program: 60 distinct hosts, or 10 distinct refused hosts, within a minute. Where the key is a service name, only the refused hosts count, because a busy page in any browser reaches 60 hosts.Warning Blocklist hitAn event a threat-intel list or a country or network rule blocked. See Threat intel.Warning for outbound; inbound drops roll up into one Info finding per list per hour Once a program or service has ten recorded events, four baseline signals join in: the first use of a port, a new public destination (after 50 connections over seven days, and not for a key that meets 20 new hosts a week), the first activity in an hour of the day, and a connection rate more than three times the usual. One signal is Info, two or more are Warning, and a baseline finding never carries a rule: an unusual connection is not answered with an allow, and a block is your decision. Two different checks on the same subject within an hour make both findings Critical. The list holds at most 200 findings. Verified test-core.sh The baseline is written to behavior.csv by the five-minute save as well as on quit, so a crash loses at most five minutes of learning. It is bounded to 256 programs, 256 ports and 1,024 destinations each; a profile that hits a cap is marked partial and the novelty signals go quiet for it, because a truncated baseline cannot honestly claim “never seen before”. Policy health# A pure check over the saved policy, run when you open the page and on Check again. Coverage is exact: the same direction, every protocol and port inside, every address inside; a row that names a host by name never covers anything. CheckSeverityFix offered Duplicate: the same rule twiceInfoRemove the later copy Shadowed: an earlier rule with the same action already covers itInfoRemove the shadowed row Unreachable: an allow covered by a denyWarningNone: you meant one of the two, so both are shown No effect: outbound allows while outgoing is allowed by defaultInfoNone Broad: an inbound allow from anyone over more than 100 portsWarningNarrow to the local network Risky service open to anyone: FTP, Telnet, RPC, NetBIOS, SMB, SQL Server, Docker, MySQL, RDP, PostgreSQL, VNC, Redis, Elasticsearch or MongoDB (SSH is Info)CriticalNarrow to the local network Reaches no family: a host name on Windows, a forward to a name or a rangeWarning for a deny, which silently blocks nothingNone Every fix is tried by the What-If engine before it is offered: a set of probes is answered against the policy as it is and with the fix made. A removal is offered only if no verdict changes; a narrowing only if every local-network probe keeps its answer and the outside probe goes from ALLOWED to BLOCKED. Otherwise the finding says “No fix offered” and why, as the note in the screenshot does. Fix shows the before and after rows and the checks, asks, writes through the apply gate, and can be undone from Changes by content, not by position. The same check runs from the command line as stockfw copilot health. Verified test-health.c Sentinel: the one automatic action# With Detect + auto-block, the default, a beacon's deny rule can be staged into the policy and pushed through the apply gate without waiting for you. Because that is the one p"},{"t":"Country and network-owner blocking","u":"docs/country-blocking.html","s":"Protection","h":[["Where the data comes from","where-the-data-comes-from"],["What “country” means","what-country-means"],["How it will work","how-it-will-work"],["What it is not","what-it-is-not"]],"x":"Country and network-owner blocking Some servers have no business talking to any country but their own, and some attacks come from a handful of networks. StockFW has two more kinds of threat-intel set, built from an offline database rather than a feed: every address registered to a country, and every address announced by a network owner (an autonomous system). Linux Country and network-owner sets are enforced on Linux, through the same nftables table as the blocklists. On Windows the Countries and networks card says “Blocking by country or network owner is not supported on Windows in this version. Blocklists are.”, and shows no control that acts. Known gap Where the data comes from# The IPFire Location database: one file, about 6 MB compressed, published daily, no account needed, with IPv4 and IPv6 in one structure. For every range it gives the country of registration, the announcing network (AS number and name), and flags for anonymous proxies, satellite providers and anycast networks. It is the same database the IPFire firewall uses for its own country blocking. The file is signed inside its header with ECDSA over a SHA-256 of its contents, and StockFW verifies that signature against a pinned public key before reading a single record. A version check uses a DNS record the publisher maintains, and a downloaded file older than the one already on disk is refused, so a replayed old database cannot roll the data back. The reader unpacks with a strict size cap and bounds-checks every offset in the header before it trusts it. The database is published under the Creative Commons Attribution-ShareAlike 4.0 licence, as IPFire confirmed to the project. StockFW credits IPFire on the Threat Intel page and in stockfw intel feeds, and never changes the data it serves. What “country” means# The country is where the address block is registered, from the regional registries, geofeeds and the publisher’s corrections. It is not a measurement of where a packet came from, and for a content network that announces one address from forty cities it cannot be. StockFW therefore says “registered in”, never “located in”, and the picker warns when a chosen country’s ranges include anycast networks: blocking such a country can block a service everyone uses, from everywhere. The same honesty applies to network owners: the AS name is the owner on record. How it will work# On the Threat Intel page, a picker with autocompletion over countries and network owners, each with its flag and the number of ranges it would add. A chosen entry becomes a set with a direction, like a feed. The sets are built, subtracted, loaded and reported exactly like blocklists: the same protected ranges, the same exceptions, the same transaction into inet stockfw_intel, the same state table, the same events and audit lines. A country block that would include your own network, your DNS servers or your SSH peer loses those addresses and says so in the notes. The lookup box, the What-if simulator and the Events page name the country or owner that blocked an address. stockfw intel gains the country and owner commands, with --json. The database updates on the same hourly check as the feeds, from the publisher only, after the same consent dialog. What it is not# Not a geofence for a laptop that travels, and not a substitute for authentication: an attacker who wants to reach you from a blocked country rents a server in an allowed one. It removes background noise and narrows the field, which is worth having, and the app will describe it that way."},{"t":"Dashboard","u":"docs/dashboard.html","s":"Command line","h":[["Start it","start-it"],["The link works once","link"],["What is on the page","what-is-on-the-page"],["Backfill and monitoring without root","backfill-and-monitoring-without-root"],["Why it is safe to run","why-it-is-safe-to-run"],["WSL","wsl"],["Over SSH","ssh"],["The API","the-api"]],"x":"Dashboard A single web page, served by the stockfw binary itself, that shows the firewall’s state, the two Activity graphs, live events, connections, the policy and the what-if simulator in a browser. It is a window onto the firewall, not a way to change it. Linux Light theme. The page is one HTML file compiled into the binary; nothing is loaded from anywhere else. Start it# TerminalCopy$ stockfw dashboard StockFW dashboard http://127.0.0.1:43977/#code=312fe461dddc3119a4c28c0ae4e40b59 Listening 127.0.0.1:43977 only, read-only Link one use, for 2 minutes; `stockfw dashboard link' makes a new one Fingerprint EA07-05C5-61E0 (the page shows the same) Events The kernel log cannot be read without root privileges. Start StockFW as root to see incoming and outbound traffic. run `sudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw dashboard' for live events Open the link above in your browser. Ctrl-C stops the dashboard. $ sudo stockfw dashboard StockFW dashboard http://127.0.0.1:43981/ Listening 127.0.0.1:43981 only, read-only Link file /run/user/1000/stockfw-dashboard-5V1UX3/open.html open it in your browser: one use, for 2 minutes Fingerprint 587A-CB3E-C247 (the page shows the same) Serves uid 1000 only (SUDO_UID) Events Live, with history from the kernel log Without --port the system picks a free port. Run it as yourself, or with sudo from your own account for live events; as plain root it refuses to start and says why, because a browser does not run as root. Under sudo the browser is not started as root, so you open the link yourself, and the dashboard serves the account that ran sudo. --no-open never starts a browser. Ctrl-C stops it, and so does closing the terminal; nothing is left behind. The link works once# The link holds a code that works once, for two minutes. It travels in the URL fragment, which browsers never send to a server. The page swaps it for a session, a key it keeps in session storage and sends with every API call; reads keep working for 12 hours, so a monitoring tab lasts the day. The link is printed only when the output is a terminal. Piped, or with --json, you get the path of a launch file instead, a private file (0600) in your runtime directory that sends the browser on to the link, so the code never ends up in a log or in an argument list. A browser that is started gets that file, as before. The launch file is deleted once its code is used, or after two minutes. TerminalCopy$ stockfw dashboard | cat StockFW dashboard http://127.0.0.1:43978/ Listening 127.0.0.1:43978 only, read-only Link file /run/user/1000/stockfw-dashboard-YREAY3/open.html open it in your browser: one use, for 2 minutes Fingerprint 65C1-2C66-305A (the page shows the same) $ stockfw dashboard --json --no-open {\"url\":\"http://127.0.0.1:43979/\",\"port\":43979,\"link_file\":\"/run/user/1000/stockfw-dashboard-8BC5X3/open.html\",\"fingerprint\":\"43D6-26AA-4862\",\"monitoring\":false} $ stockfw dashboard link http://127.0.0.1:43980/#code=796e07cec3c56f510f01df6dd4fa788b One use, for 2 minutes. Fingerprint B150-4979-89E1. When the link has expired, a tab was closed, or you want a second tab, stockfw dashboard link asks the running dashboard for a fresh link, over a socket only your account can use; with more than one dashboard running it needs --port N. A link used twice gets a “link used” page, ends every session, and the terminal says so: a second use means someone else saw the link. The fingerprint is shown in the terminal and at the top of the page, so you can check that the page you are looking at came from your dashboard and not from another local server. Use the address as printed, http://127.0.0.1:port; localhost is not accepted. Verified test-dashboard.sh What is on the page# Tiles: firewall state (and whether IPv6 is in force), blocked and allowed counts for the chosen range, throughput now. Range picker, 15 minutes to 24 hours, and Policy activity and Network I/O graphs drawn by the same code as the Activity page, with the same no-data band, crosshair and keyboard navigation. Recent events, newest first, with a filter box, and Active connections with their programs. The dashboard hides its own sockets: they exist only because someone is looking. What-if simulator: the same grammar and verdicts as the app and the command line. Nothing is written. Policy: the saved rules, grouped by kind, with their Covers. Threat intel: whether enforcement is on, each enabled feed with its last update, and how many ranges the kernel holds. The fingerprint of this run, at the top, to compare with the terminal; and a theme button: system, light or dark. Dark theme. The graph colours were checked for contrast in both. Backfill and monitoring without root# The app’s Activity graphs start when the app starts. The dashboard can read the kernel log for the events StockFW’s rules logged before it was started, so its history begins before the browser was opened, and the no-data band is shorter or absent. The log stays the archive; the dashboard only reads it. Started as your own user, the dashboard follows a kernel log file your account can read, such as /var/log/kern.log for a user in the adm group, so live events do not always need sudo; when nothing is readable the terminal says so and prints the sudo command to run. Why it is safe to run# A page that shows your firewall is a page an attacker would like to read, or to use as a lever. The dashboard is designed so that neither works, and a test exercises each point: the project’s dashboard suite makes the requests as the served account, as root and as another user, and checks what each gets. Verified test-dashboard.sh Verified test-cli.sh Loopback only, and only your account It binds 127.0.0.1; there is no option to listen anywhere else, and asking for --bind prints why. Before a byte is read, the dashboard asks the kernel who owns the connecting socket: it must come from 127.0.0.1 exactly and belong to the account being served, never to root or to any other account. Anything else is closed at once, unread, even with a valid link. To use it from another machine, forward the port over SSH. A one-use link and a session The code is 128 bits from the kernel’s random source, good for one use within two minutes; the session key is 256 bits, lives in the process’s memory only, and is sent in an X-StockFW-Session header compared in constant time. There are no cookies. At most four sessions are open at once, reads work for 12 hours, and a restart ends them all. A code used twice ends every session. Host check Exactly one Host header, equal to 127.0.0.1:port, else the request is refused before any path is looked at. localhost is refused on purpose: the server binds IPv4 only, another account could listen on [::1] at the same port, and a browser sent to localhost may go there first. A hostile name re-pointed at 127.0.0.1, the DNS-rebinding trick, fails the same check. The page’s own requests only The one POST, which swaps the code for a session, must carry the page’s own Origin, Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin and a JSON body, which a form on another site cannot send. An API request that names another site in Origin is refused even with a valid session, and OPTIONS gets 405 with no CORS header, ever, so another site open in the same browser cannot read the API or ride the session. Read-only The API has no write endpoint. Changing the firewall stays with stockfw apply and the desktop app, which need root; the dashboard can be left running without that worry. Nothing from elsewhere The page is compiled into the binary and carries a strict Content-Security-Policy with a per-run nonce and Trusted Types, a Permissions-Policy that denies camera, microphone, location and clipboard reading, and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy. No external script, style, font or image can load; the page builds every node from text and never parses HTML. It works offline and sets no cookies. Slow senders cannot lock you out The request head must arrive within five seconds and a body within five more; a body is at"},{"t":"Events","u":"docs/events.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Where the events come from","where-the-events-come-from"],["The columns","the-columns"],["How many it keeps","how-many-it-keeps"],["Search, filters, export","search-filters-export"],["History on disk","history"],["Repeated events and noise","repeated-events-and-noise"],["Right-click: from an event to a rule","right-click-from-an-event-to-a-rule"],["What else reads the events","what-else-reads-the-events"]],"x":"Events Every connection the firewall allowed or blocked, as it happens. StockFW does not capture packets; it reads the verdicts your operating system already records and shows them unchanged, so each row can be checked against the system’s own log. [[shot:events|The Events page: a count of 12 events, a search box, the Monitor switch, Clear and Refresh, a filter bar with direction, verdict, protocol, port, address, From and To, Reset and Export CSV, and a table of time, direction, interfaces, port, source, destination, protocol and service with blocked rows in red.|Blocked rows are red and carry the [BLOCKED] marker that every other part of the app keys on. The filter bar narrows the list by direction, verdict, protocol, port, address and time.]] Where the events come from# Windows The Security event log, events 5156 (the Windows Filtering Platform permitted a connection) and 5157 (it blocked one). StockFW runs wevtutil from the system folder every five seconds and reads every record after the last one it delivered, up to 10,000 a poll, newest first and shown oldest first, so a burst arrives whole. Past that cap the newest 10,000 are read and the gap is counted, as an upper bound, next to the event count (“up to N skipped”). Each record carries the program path, both addresses, the ports, the protocol and the direction, so the Events page on Windows names the program; the In and Out columns hold this computer’s address and the other side’s. The audit policy that makes Windows write these events is switched on the first time the elevated app runs; see Enable monitoring. Linux The kernel log. StockFW’s own rules log what they drop and reject with the prefixes STOCKFW-DROP and STOCKFW-REJECT, and, when Log allowed connections is on, the first packet of each newly allowed connection with STOCKFW-ALLOW, rate-limited to ten a second. A block by a threat-intel list or a country or network rule logs with STOCKFW-TI and the list’s id, so the row names the list as its rule. The page reads those lines from the kernel ring buffer (/dev/kmsg) directly, which needs root and works on WSL, in containers and on minimal systems, and it never skips a line; lines without a StockFW prefix are ignored, so unrelated kernel messages never appear. The kernel log carries no program name, so there is no Program column on Linux; the Overview’s connection table names programs instead. Known gap The columns# Time (the event’s own time, in local HH:MM:SS), direction (in or out), the interfaces in and out (on Windows, this computer’s address and the other side’s), the local port, source and destination addresses, protocol, and the service name looked up from the port, with [BLOCKED] appended on a blocked row and, after it, the rule that blocked it when a list did. On Windows there is also the program. Every column sorts, and rows are inserted newest first by their time stamp, so an event that arrives late still lands in the right place. Verified ui-hitview-smoke How many it keeps# The list holds 100,000 events by default and up to 1,000,000 (Preferences › Events › Events kept in the list), and stays responsive at that size: the rows live in a ring buffer outside the toolkit, each string is kept once (about 75 bytes an event, so 100,000 is about 7 MB), the view copies no rows, and a live event under any sort order moves at most one small block. The oldest event goes first. The same row twice in a row is one event. Verified ui-hitview-smoke Search, filters, export# Search narrows the list as you type: a case-insensitive match against any column the list shows, the blocking list’s name included. The count reads “3 of 1,200 events” while a filter is active, so an empty result is never mistaken for an idle firewall. Verified test_filter_match The filter bar adds direction, verdict (blocked, allowed or both), protocol, a port or a range (1000-2000), an address or a CIDR block (10.0.0.0/8, fd00::/8), and a time range (From and To). Every filter must match. Reset clears them all. The command line has the same filters: stockfw events --since 1d --port 22 --dir in. Export CSV writes the rows that match the current filters to a file only your account can read (RFC 4180; a cell a spreadsheet would run as a formula is written as text). Monitor starts and stops reading the log. When it cannot start, because you are not root on Linux or the audit policy is off on Windows, the page says so and how to fix it instead of showing nothing. Clear empties the list and forgets which rows it has seen; the history on disk, if you keep one, is not touched. Refresh reads again now. An empty list always explains itself: waiting for the first event, monitoring off, or nothing matches the filter. History on disk# Keep event history on Preferences › Events is off until you turn it on. On, every event the list accepts is also appended to a file: one file per day under ~/.local/share/stockfw/events on Linux and %LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\events on Windows, readable by your account only, flushed once a second and at once when the session ends, each line with a checksum so a line cut short by a crash is skipped rather than misread. Files older than 30 days, or beyond 200 MB in all (both adjustable on the same page), are deleted oldest first; Delete history asks first and deletes only history files. When StockFW starts, the newest events are read back into the list on a worker thread, so the window is up before the history is in, and a burst that arrives while it loads still reaches the file whole. Only one copy of StockFW writes a given history; a second copy on the same account says so and saves nothing until the first is gone. stockfw events --history searches the saved files without root. Verified test-eventstore.sh Repeated events and noise# Two settings on Preferences › Events shape the list. Hide repeated events drops a row when the same source, port and verdict arrived a moment ago, so a port scan becomes a few rows rather than thousands. Skip events that are not for the firewall filters records the firewall subsystem did not produce. The Copilot is fed before these display filters, so hiding repeats never hides a scan from the detections. De-duplication happens three times for three reasons: the reader refuses to deliver the same record twice, the list refuses to insert the same row twice (so a monitor that is stopped and restarted does not repeat itself), and the preference above hides visually identical connections. Verified ui-hitview-smoke Right-click: from an event to a rule# The menu offers only what that row can express, so it can never promise a rule the policy cannot hold: Outbound rows: Block destination (all traffic), Allow destination (all traffic), and Block this service to the destination, where the service is taken from the row’s port. On a row a threat-intel list blocked, Allow becomes Add exception, because an allow rule would not beat the list. Inbound rows: Trust source (inbound allow), or Remove trust for this source when a trust rule for it already exists. There is no inbound-deny rule kind, because inbound is denied by default; removing the trust is what blocks the source again. On an inbound row the only offer is to trust the source. Blocking it is already the default. Choosing an entry opens a dialog that prints the exact rule, as it will be written to the policy file. Where the rule kind has an opposite-direction twin, the dialog also carries the Direction setting: one direction by default, or Bi-directional, which stages the twin as part of the same change. After Yes, the rule is saved (and taken back out if the save fails), the Policy page reloads, and the change is pushed through the apply gate. The result dialog distinguishes live from staged, not live yet: administrator privileges are required. Verified test_eventrule_builders Allowing a destination also lifts any block Sentinel staged for it, before the allow is pushed, so the two reach the firewall together; on Windows a block rul"},{"t":"Questions and answers","u":"docs/faq.html","s":"Reference","h":[["About the product","about-the-product"],["Reboot and persistence","reboot"],["Linux","linux"],["Windows","windows"],["Versions","versions"],["Something else?","something-else"]],"x":"Questions and answers About the product# Does StockFW replace Windows Firewall or iptables? No. It configures them. The engine your system already has keeps doing the filtering, which is why StockFW has no driver, needs no reboot, and leaves the firewall exactly as it was when it is closed, crashes or is uninstalled. Does anything leave my computer? Nothing. There is no telemetry, no update check, no account and no network client. The dashboard listens on 127.0.0.1 only. The threat-intel feature downloads lists, each only after you turn it on and accept its publisher’s terms. The security model spells this out. Is the Copilot an AI? No. It is a set of fixed rules that run on this computer over the event log and the saved policy: no model, no network, no telemetry. Every finding names the rule that raised it, the numbers it measured next to their limits, and the events behind it, and every fix it offers was checked by the What-If engine first. It works offline and cannot be talked into anything because there is no prompt. The Copilot page. Why is there no “deny inbound” rule kind? Because inbound is denied by default. Every inbound rule is an exception that allows something; to block a source again you remove the allow. Outbound is the mirror image: allowed by default, with deny rules as the exceptions, and an optional restrictive mode that flips it. Where is the Windows command line? There is none. stockfw is Linux only; on Windows, the app is the whole tool. The core it links against is portable, so the gap is a packaging one, not a design one. Known gap Which licence? The repository carries two texts: the inherited GPLv2 in COPYING for the original code, and Apache-2.0 in LICENSE. The README says which applies to what, and the question of a single licence for the whole is open. Known gap Reboot and persistence# What happens to my rules at reboot? Windows Windows Firewall rules persist; StockFW’s rules are ordinary rules in it, so they are in force from boot, before any program runs. Linux iptables rules live in the kernel and are gone at boot, so the Linux installer enables stockfw.service, which runs stockfw start before the network comes up and loads the last policy applied as root. Threat-intel lists come back with it. A policy saved but never applied waits for the next Apply. At boot has the details. Verified test-boot-start.sh Is there help inside the app? Yes. F1, or Help in the sidebar, opens a Help window at the topic for the page you are on, with a searchable list of topics, Back and Forward, and links between topics. It is built into the program and works offline. Help and About. If StockFW crashes, am I unprotected? No. The engine keeps the last applied rules. What you lose is monitoring, learning and Sentinel until you start it again. Settings and what the Copilot has learned are saved every five minutes when they change, so a crash loses at most five minutes of learning. Linux# iptables or nftables? StockFW drives iptables and ip6tables, which on every current distribution are the nftables-backed versions, so its rules land in nftables and nft list ruleset shows them. The threat-intel sets use nft directly, in their own table. The policy itself has no native nftables backend. Known gap Does it fight with ufw, firewalld, Docker or libvirt? StockFW owns its own chains (INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT, STOCKFW-SNAT) and replaces only those; rules other tools keep in the same tables are not touched, and the loads use --noflush. Two tools both setting a default policy on INPUT will still disagree about what the default is, so run one host firewall manager at a time; ufw and firewalld are alternatives to StockFW, Docker and libvirt coexist with it. Why root, and not polkit or a setuid helper? Because a desktop program that can silently rewrite the firewall is a worse posture than one that asks for root openly. StockFW never raises its own rights. More. Which distributions? Tested on Debian, Kali, Ubuntu and WSL2. Anything with GTK 3, liblzma 5, OpenSSL 3 and iptables should work; the installer checks libraries by soname, not by package name, so renamed packages do not fail it. Debian 11 and Ubuntu 20.04 ship only OpenSSL 1.1 and are refused. Windows# Why two shortcuts? The ordinary one runs with your rights and can read, edit and simulate; the elevated one asks for administrator rights once and can change the firewall. Keeping them separate means a program running as you cannot borrow the elevated window. Install on Windows. Why is the installer not signed? A code-signing certificate is a cost the project has not taken on yet. Check the SHA-256 on the download page instead; it tells you the same thing a signature would. Known gap Does it work with a third-party antivirus firewall? StockFW manages the Windows engine. If another product has replaced Windows Firewall as the active firewall, StockFW’s rules exist but that product decides. Versions# Which version do these pages describe? 1.0.8, the release on the download page. The app shows its version in the sidebar and in About StockFW, whose Copy system info copies it with the GTK version, the platform and the backend; stockfw version prints it. What do the stamps on these pages mean? “Verified” names an automated test that proves the sentence, “Measured” gives a number from an experiment that was run, and “Limit” marks something StockFW does not do. The welcome page explains each. Something else?# If the troubleshooting page does not have it, open an issue. Questions are welcome there too; they become documentation."},{"t":"Files and formats","u":"docs/files.html","s":"Reference","h":[["Where","where"],["The policy","policy-files"],["The applied journal","applied-csv"],["Preferences","stockfw-conf"],["The audit log","audit-log"],["The event history","event-history"],["The Trust baseline","trust-csv"],["The behaviour baseline","behavior-csv"],["Sentinel’s staging record","autoblocks"],["The threat-intel store","intel-store"],["Start at sign-in","sign-in"],["In the engine","in-the-engine"],["Backing up","backing-up"]],"x":"Files and formats StockFW keeps everything as plain text you can read, copy, diff and back up. There is no database and no registry blob. This page is the format reference; nothing here is needed to use the app. Where# PlatformPolicy and journalPreferences, logs, baselines Windows%LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\%APPDATA%\\stockfw\\ Linux, as your user~/.stockfw/~/.config/stockfw/ Linux, as root/etc/stockfw//root/.config/stockfw/ STOCKFW_RULES_DIR overrides the first column; XDG_CONFIG_HOME the second on Linux; and STOCKFW_AUDIT_FILE, STOCKFW_TRUST_FILE and STOCKFW_AUTOBLOCK_FILE point single files elsewhere. Three more places hold the rest: WhatWindowsLinux Event history%LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\events\\~/.local/share/stockfw/events/ (STOCKFW_EVENTS_DIR overrides) Threat-intel store%ProgramData%\\StockFW\\intel\\/var/lib/stockfw/intel/ Start at sign-inTask Scheduler folder \\StockFW, one task per user~/.config/autostart/stockfw.desktop; the boot service is /etc/systemd/system/stockfw.service The policy# One file per rule kind, no extension, under inbound/ and outbound/. A file that does not exist is an empty kind. FileColumns inbound/allow-fromhost, comment inbound/allow-serviceservice, port, for, comment inbound/forwardservice, firewall port, to, port, comment outbound/allow-to, outbound/allow-from, outbound/deny-to, outbound/deny-fromhost, comment outbound/allow-service, outbound/deny-serviceservice, port, for, comment inbound/allow-serviceCopySSH, 22, 192.168.1.0/24, admin LAN HTTPS, 443, Anyone, web server inbound/forwardCopyWeb, 8080, 192.168.1.30, 80, lab box Comma-separated, one rule per line, a space after each comma on write and optional on read. The comment is the last field and may be empty. A field may not contain a comma or a line break, and the app refuses to save one that does, because it would become another field. Ports are digits only (a range is 1024:2048); a host is an address, a CIDR network, Anyone, or a host name that does not start with a hyphen. A line that does not parse is skipped at load and counted (“2 malformed policy rules were skipped”); saving writes back only the rules that parsed, and never rewrites a bad line into something else. Verified test_policy_load_validation_f1 Round trip is exact: what you save is what loads. You may edit these files by hand with the app closed; the Policy page shows the result, and nothing reaches the firewall until Apply. The applied journal# applied.csv next to the policy: the last policy the engine accepted. Apply measures its impact against this file, not against the saved policy, and rolls back to it when the engine refuses. applied.csvCopy# StockFW applied policy journal v1 inbound-allow-service, \"SSH, 22, 192.168.1.0/24, admin LAN\" outbound-deny-to, \"198.51.100.66, known bad host\" One row per applied rule: the kind, then the rule’s own line as one quoted field. A row that cannot be trusted fails the whole load, and an apply then refuses rather than roll back to something unverifiable; the dialog says so. The file is rewritten after every successful apply. If it is missing, the next apply counts every rule as added, which is also what happens on a fresh install. Verified test_apply_gate Preferences# stockfw.conf, an ini file with two groups. Keys keep the names the original program used, so a setting is easy to find: stockfw.confCopy[client] first_run=false enable_tray_icon=true minimize_to_tray=true filter/redundant=false filter/not_for_firewall=true events/max_events=100000 events/keep_history=false events/history_days=30 events/history_mb=200 policy_auto_apply=true policy_audit_only=false start_firewall_on_gui=false [firewall] ext_if=eth0 int_if= nat=false icmp/enable=true icmp/echo_request=true deny_packets=true block_external_broadcast=false block_internal_broadcast=false block_non_routables=true restrictive_outbound=false log_allowed=true A missing key takes its default. The file is written when you press Save, every five minutes while StockFW runs if something changed, and when the session ends. The Preferences page describes each setting. The audit log# audit.log, append-only, one line per thing StockFW did to the firewall or the baselines: audit.logCopy2026-11-02T16:40:12Z | policy applied | source=Policy page mode=live +1 added (1 allow, 0 block), -0 removed (0 allow, 0 block), =8 unchanged 2026-11-02T16:41:03Z | policy held, firewall untouched | source=cli mode=audit-only +0 added ... =9 unchanged 2026-11-02T16:44:50Z | event rule added | outbound-deny-to 203.0.113.9 (Events page) live 2026-11-02T16:51:19Z | sentinel auto-block | outbound-deny-to 198.51.100.77 beacon 6 contacts, mean 30 s 2026-11-02T17:01:19Z | sentinel rule removed | outbound-deny-to 198.51.100.77: expired after 10 minutes 2026-11-02T17:05:02Z | trust-attest | /usr/bin/nc.openbsd sha256=… The three fields are the time in UTC, the action, and the detail, separated by | . Actions include policy applied, policy applied, with notes, policy apply failed, policy apply refused, policy apply: no change, policy held, firewall untouched, event rule added, event rule added (bi-directional twin), event trust removed, the sentinel … family and trust-attest / trust-forget. Line breaks and the separator inside a detail are escaped, so a tool’s error text can never read as a second, forged entry. The app only ever appends; rotation is yours. Verified test_apply_gate The event history# Written only while Keep event history is on. One file per UTC day, split into parts so no single file grows past a twentieth of the size cap: events-2026-11-03-001.log, the part number three digits or more. Each line is one event, tab-separated, with a checksum at the end: events-2026-11-03-001.logCopyticks time direction in out port source destination length tos protocol service rule checksum ticks is the time in 100-nanosecond units since 1970 UTC; text fields escape backslash, tab and line breaks; the checksum is FNV-1a 32 of everything before it, in hex. A line cut short by a crash fails its checksum and is skipped, and the next writer starts a fresh line. The folder is created owner-only, and one copy of StockFW at a time holds a lock on .lock inside it, so no event is saved twice. Files older than the day limit, and the oldest files past the size cap, are deleted when the log opens or passes the cap. stockfw events --history reads these files; see History on disk. Verified test-eventstore.sh The Trust baseline# trust.csv: quoted CSV, eight fields per row, one row per attested path. trust.csvCopy\"C:\\Program Files\\Mozilla Firefox\\firefox.exe\",\"9f3b…c1\",\"68425616\",\"1761840000\",\"valid\",\"Mozilla Corporation\",\"1762085102\",\"1762091840\" Path, SHA-256, size, modification time (unix seconds), signature state (valid, invalid, unsigned, na, unknown), publisher, first seen, last seen. A row with a different number of fields is skipped, never the file. Verified test_trust_engine The behaviour baseline# behavior.csv: what the Copilot has learned, one fact per row, keyed by program. behavior.csvCopyfirefox,meta,1342,98304,2211840,1762080000,1762091830 firefox,rate,1762091400,7,6250,41,0 firefox,port,443,1288 firefox,port,80,41 firefox,dest,104.16.4.34,212 firefox,hour,14,301 curl,partial,1 Row kinds: meta (totals and first/last seen), rate (the connection-frequency state), port, dest and hour (counts), and partial (the profile was capped and can no longer claim novelty). Rows of an unknown kind are ignored, so an older version can read a newer file. Delete the file to start learning from scratch. Sentinel’s staging record# autoblocks: what Sentinel has staged and when, rewritten on every stage and every retirement. autoblocksCopyS,1762094279,7,198.51.100.77, Sentinel: beacon 6 contacts Four fields: S, the staging time in unix seconds, the rule kind’s number, and the rule’s own CSV line last, so its commas survive the split. At start-up, rows older than ten minutes are taken out of the policy with an audit line; fresh rows are re-armed with their original time. Delet"},{"t":"Your first ten minutes","u":"docs/first-steps.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Where to go next","where-to-go-next"]],"x":"Your first ten minutes This walk-through uses the real app on a small network, so what you see here is what you will see on your own computer. Start the app with the rights to change the firewall: StockFW (Elevated) on Windows, sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW on Linux. Read the Overview The green or red card at the top answers the question you opened the app for. On a fresh install it says the firewall is stopped; press Start firewall. The card turns green, and the two small tiles say whether IPv4 and IPv6 traffic are filtered. On Linux, if ip6tables is missing, the IPv6 tile tells you instead of pretending. After Start: protected, both families filtered, the policy in force. Watch the Events page for a minute Every connection the firewall decided appears as it happens. Red rows were blocked. Type part of an address, a port or a program name into the filter and the list narrows while you type; the count reads “3 of 1200 events” so an empty filter result cannot be mistaken for a quiet firewall. The filter bar also narrows by direction, verdict, protocol, port and address. If the list stays empty, it tells you why: monitoring is off, or nothing matched. Turn an event into a rule Right-click a row. For an outbound connection you can block or allow the destination, or block just that service to it; for an inbound one you can trust the source. A dialog prints the exact rule it is about to write, you answer Yes or No, and the result says plainly whether the rule is live or only saved for the next Apply. The right-click menu offers only what that row can express. Add a rule by hand Open Policy. Rules are grouped by kind. In Allow inbound service, press Add, choose SSH on port 22 and type your LAN, for example 192.168.1.0/24, as who it is for. The row appears with its Covers column reading “IPv4 only”, because that network is an IPv4 network. A rule for “Anyone” covers both families. Each rule says what it reaches, and why on hover. Apply, and read the impact first Press Apply policy. Before anything happens, a dialog counts what will change against what is actually live: “+1 added (1 allow, 0 block), −0 removed, =8 unchanged”. Say Yes and the rule reaches the firewall; the result dialog reports what the engine said. If the engine refuses, the last good policy is put back and the dialog says whether that worked. Every attempt is one line in audit.log. Not ready to change anything? Press Audit-only first. Apply then stages the change and writes the audit line while the firewall stays exactly as it is. Ask the simulator Open Copilot and type a question into the What-If card the way you would say it: inbound rdp from 203.0.113.50. Read as shows how the line was understood, and the answer appears in one row: ALLOWED or BLOCKED, and the rule that decides it, or that no rule matched and the default applied. Details opens the window beside the page: the packet travelling from the source to the wall, every rule that was checked, and, under What you can do, the fixes StockFW has verified, such as the one allow rule that would let this in, printed exactly as it would be written. Asking writes nothing; a fix runs only when you press its button and confirm. A question, not a change: nothing is written by asking, and the offered fix shows its row before you decide. Let it run in the background Open Preferences from the bottom of the sidebar. On the General page, Show a tray icon and Close and minimize to the tray keep StockFW running when you close the window, and Start StockFW in the background when I sign in brings it back at your next sign-in. The tray icon’s tooltip carries the firewall state, so a glance answers the question the app exists for. Press F1 on any page for the built-in help. Where to go next# Policy explains every rule kind, what “Covers” means, and what Apply does step by step. Copilot explains insights, Sentinel and auto-block, and why none of it can open a port without you. On Linux, the command line does everything above from a terminal or a script, and the dashboard shows it in a browser."},{"t":"Help and About","u":"docs/help.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["The Help window","the-help-window"],["Keyboard shortcuts","keyboard-shortcuts"],["About","about"]],"x":"Help and About StockFW carries its own help. It is a window, not a web page: every topic is compiled into the program, nothing is fetched, and it works on a computer with no Internet connection. This site covers the same ground at more length; the Help window is what you reach for while the app is in front of you. The Help window# The window opens at Getting started, or at the topic for the page you pressed F1 on. Open it with F1 from any page of the main window and it lands on the topic for that page. The Help item at the bottom of the sidebar, the Help button and F1 in Preferences (the topic for the settings page shown), and Help in the tray menu (the topic on running in the background) open the same window. Topics: Getting started; Overview and status, Events, Activity graphs, Policy, Threat intel, Copilot, What-If, Sentinel and Trust; Preferences with one topic per settings page; Running in the background; Command line and dashboard; Linux notes and Windows notes; Troubleshooting, Keyboard shortcuts, and About and credits. Search looks through titles, summaries, text and extra keywords, and Enter opens the first topic found. Back and Forward, also Alt+Left and Alt+Right, keep a history as you follow links between topics. A link to anything outside the help opens in your browser. There is one Help window. It is not modal: it takes input while Preferences is open, stays open when Preferences closes, and from the tray it opens on its own with the main window left hidden. The help describes the build in front of you. It is checked against the program: a smoke test opens every topic, follows every link, searches, walks Back and Forward, presses F1 on every page including Threat Intel, and reads the sentences the release reviews corrected, so the help cannot drift from the code without a test failing. Verified fs-help-smoke Keyboard shortcuts# WhereKeysWhat they do Main windowF1Help for the page you are on Ctrl+,Preferences Ctrl+QQuit StockFW Alt+APolicy: apply the policy Alt+CEvents: clear the list Alt+FEvents: read recent events again Left Right Home EndActivity: read one interval of the graph at a time EnterTrust: inspect the path you typed PreferencesF1Help for the page shown EscapeCancel and close HelpCtrl+FSearch the help EscapeClear the search EnterOpen the first topic found Alt+Left, Alt+RightBack, Forward Ctrl+WClose Help AboutEscapeClose it About# About: the credits, the licence summary with both texts one click away, and Copy system info. About StockFW at the bottom of the sidebar shows the version, the credits and a licence summary that is true to the source: the tree carries the GNU General Public License version 2 (COPYING) and the Apache License 2.0 (LICENSE), the code it was ported from was released under the GPL version 2 or any later version, and which licence covers which part is still under review. View licence texts shows both texts, compiled into the program. The installers ship the same summary followed by both texts, and a test reads the staged file back. Verified test-licence-texts.sh Copy system info puts the StockFW and GTK versions, the platform and the firewall backend on the clipboard, for a bug report. It copies nothing about you: no user name, host name or home folder. The About window is not modal and there is one at a time."},{"t":"Install on Linux","u":"docs/install-linux.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Before you start","before-you-start"],["Install","install"],["What gets installed","what-gets-installed"],["Run it","run-it"],["Why the full path after sudo","why-the-full-path-after-sudo"],["Why there is no polkit policy or setuid helper","why-there-is-no-polkit-policy-or-setuid-helper"],["At boot","at-boot"],["Two homes: yours and root’s","two-homes-yours-and-root-s"],["Where StockFW keeps its files","where-stockfw-keeps-its-files"],["Events on Linux","events-on-linux"],["Update","update"],["Uninstall","uninstall"],["Build it yourself","build-it-yourself"]],"x":"Install on Linux The Linux build is one tarball of about 1.2 MB: the desktop app, the stockfw command line with its manual page and shell completion, a boot service, a menu entry, the README, the changelog, the licence texts and an install script. GTK comes from your distribution. Before you start# An x86-64 distribution with GTK 3. Debian, Kali, Ubuntu and WSL2 are what it is tested on. liblzma 5 and OpenSSL 3 (liblzma.so.5 and libcrypto.so.3), which read the signed location database. A distribution that ships only OpenSSL 1.1, such as Debian 11 or Ubuntu 20.04, is refused by the installer before anything is copied, because the app and the boot service could not start there. iptables and ip6tables, and nft and curl if you want threat-intel blocklists or country blocking. On current distributions iptables is the nftables-backed version, which is fine: StockFW’s rules land in nftables through it. Root, for reading the kernel log and for changing rules. Everything else works as your own user. Install# TerminalCopy$ sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt --ignore-missing StockFW-1.0.8-linux-x86_64.tar.gz: OK $ tar xzf StockFW-1.0.8-linux-x86_64.tar.gz $ cd StockFW-1.0.8-linux-x86_64 $ sudo ./install.sh The script checks that every library the binary links against is present, by soname rather than by package name, because Debian and Ubuntu renamed libgtk-3-0 to libgtk-3-0t64 and a package check would wrongly fail a working system. If something is missing, it names the library and stops: TerminalCopy$ sudo apt install libgtk-3-0 libgdk-pixbuf-2.0-0 liblzma5 libssl3 # Debian, Kali, Ubuntu $ sudo dnf install gtk3 xz-libs openssl-libs # Fedora $ sudo pacman -S gtk3 xz openssl # Arch iptables, nft and curl are checked separately and only warned about: without iptables the app still runs, reads, simulates and shows the policy, and only applying needs it; without nft or curl the threat-intel lists cannot be turned on, and the Threat Intel page says so. What gets installed# PathWhat /usr/local/bin/StockFWThe desktop application. /usr/local/bin/stockfwThe command line and local dashboard. The two names differ only in case; they are staged apart in the tarball so a case-insensitive filesystem cannot merge them. /usr/local/share/man/man1/stockfw.1The manual page, man stockfw, written by the tool itself from its command tables. /usr/local/share/bash-completion/completions/stockfw, /usr/local/share/zsh/site-functions/_stockfwCompletion for bash and zsh: commands, options, values and your own rule references. See the command line page. /usr/local/share/applications/stockfw.desktopThe menu entry (Settings › StockFW). It starts the unprivileged app. /usr/local/share/icons/hicolor/{48x48,128x128,256x256}/apps/stockfw.pngIcons. /etc/systemd/system/stockfw.serviceThe boot service, enabled by the installer: it runs stockfw start before the network comes up and loads the last applied policy, so the rules are back after a restart. See At boot. /usr/local/share/doc/stockfw/LICENSE.txt, CHANGELOG.mdThe licence summary with both licence texts, and the changelog. /usr/local/bin/stockfw-uninstallRemoves all of the above. Run it# TerminalCopy$ StockFW # as yourself: policy, simulator, Trust; no events, no apply $ sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW # as root: the full tool As your own user, the app can show and edit a policy, run the simulator and inspect programs, but the kernel log is unreadable without root, so the Events page says that monitoring is unavailable rather than showing an empty list, and Apply refuses with a message rather than half-applying. Why the full path after sudo# sudo does not search your PATH. It uses Defaults secure_path from /etc/sudoers, which on Fedora, RHEL and some Kali systems is /usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin and does not include /usr/local/bin (Ubuntu’s default does). There, sudo StockFW reports “command not found” while plain StockFW works. The installer reads secure_path in both the quoted form Debian writes and the unquoted form RHEL and Fedora write, and warns in its summary when the path is missing. It deliberately does not edit sudoers. Adding /usr/local/bin to secure_path would widen the search path for every command anyone runs under sudo on that machine, which is a worse trade than typing a path. Why there is no polkit policy or setuid helper# StockFW expects to already be root when it changes the firewall and never re-executes itself through sudo. A desktop program that can silently rewrite the whole firewall is a worse security posture than one that asks for root openly, so the installer ships neither a polkit rule nor a setuid wrapper. Grant privilege by launching with sudo, or through your desktop environment’s own mechanism. At boot# iptables rules live in the kernel only, so a reboot empties them. The installer puts in and enables stockfw.service, which runs stockfw start once, before any network interface comes up, so the machine is never on the network unfiltered between boot and sign-in. It starts the last policy that was applied as root (/etc/stockfw/applied.csv): a policy that was saved but never applied, or held by Audit-only or the Lock, waits for the next Apply, and on a machine where nobody has applied a policy yet the service does nothing. There is no stop action on purpose: stopping the unit or shutting down never takes the rules away; sudo stockfw stop does. A deny rule that names a host by name cannot be looked up before the network is up, so it is left out at boot, journalctl -u stockfw says which, and it goes in with the next apply. Verified test-boot-start.sh The policy edited in StockFW started without sudo lives in ~/.stockfw and is not what the service loads; see the next section. Two homes: yours and root’s# The policy lives in a directory that depends on who is running the program: as your user: ~/.stockfw/, with preferences and logs in ~/.config/stockfw/; as root: /etc/stockfw/, with preferences and logs in /root/.config/stockfw/. A rule you add as yourself is therefore not the rule sudo stockfw apply applies. Pick one identity for policy work and stay with it: the simplest is to run both the app and every stockfw policy command through sudo, so everything lives in /etc/stockfw. For scripts and tests, STOCKFW_RULES_DIR=/some/dir points both programs at one directory regardless of who runs them. Where StockFW keeps its files# File (as root)Holds /etc/stockfw/inbound/…, outbound/…The policy, one file per rule kind. See Files and formats. /etc/stockfw/applied.csvThe last policy the engine accepted: what Apply measures against and rolls back to. /root/.config/stockfw/stockfw.confPreferences. /root/.config/stockfw/audit.logOne line per apply, attest, forget and Sentinel action. Append-only. /root/.config/stockfw/trust.csv, behavior.csv, autoblocksThe Trust baseline, the Copilot’s learned behaviour, Sentinel’s staged blocks. In the kernel, StockFW owns the chains INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT and STOCKFW-SNAT, hooked from INPUT, OUTPUT, PREROUTING and POSTROUTING. On each apply only those chains are replaced; rules that Docker, libvirt or ufw keep in the same tables are not touched. Events on Linux# The policy logs what it drops, rejects and, with Log allowed connections on, newly allows, and the Events page reads those lines from the kernel ring buffer (/dev/kmsg) directly, so it works on WSL, in containers and on systems without rsyslog or journald. Root is required. Update# Unpack the new tarball and run its install.sh again. Your policy, preferences and event history are outside /usr/local and are kept; the boot service is replaced. Uninstall# TerminalCopy$ sudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw-uninstall Removes exactly what the installer created: the binaries, the boot service (disabled and deleted), the menu entry, the icons, the licence and the changelog, and your own start-at-sign-in entry. A running StockFW is asked to quit first so it saves its settings. It leaves your policy in /etc/stockfw or ~/.stockfw, your preferences and event"},{"t":"Install on Windows","u":"docs/install-windows.html","s":"Get started","h":[["Before you start","before-you-start"],["Run the installer","run-the-installer"],["What gets installed","what-gets-installed"],["Two ways to run it","two-ways-to-run-it"],["Normal: the “StockFW” shortcut","normal-the-stockfw-shortcut"],["Elevated: the “StockFW (Elevated)” shortcut","elevated-the-stockfw-elevated-shortcut"],["Enable monitoring","enable-monitoring"],["Where StockFW keeps its files","where-stockfw-keeps-its-files"],["Update","update"],["Uninstall","uninstall"],["Build it yourself","build-it-yourself"]],"x":"Install on Windows StockFW ships as a single installer that includes everything it needs. Installing takes a minute; the only decision is where to put it. Before you start# Windows 10 or Windows 11, 64-bit. Windows Defender Firewall must exist. It always does, even when another security product is installed; StockFW manages the Windows engine, not a third-party one. An administrator account for installing, and for changing rules later. Reading events and editing the policy on disk do not need it. Run the installer# Download and check itGet StockFW-1.0.8-Setup.exe and SHA256SUMS.txt from the download page. In PowerShell, Get-FileHash .\\StockFW-1.0.8-Setup.exe must print the hash listed for it. Get past SmartScreenThe installer is not code-signed yet, so Windows shows “Windows protected your PC”. Choose More info, then Run anyway. You checked the hash; that is what the signature would have told you. Read the licence and choose a folderThe licence page shows the same summary as the app’s About window, followed by both licence texts in full. The default folder is C:\\Program Files\\StockFW. Keep it under Program Files if you can: that folder is writable only by administrators, which is part of what makes the elevated launcher safe to use. Choose componentsMain Application is required. Desktop Shortcut adds a shortcut to the ordinary, non-elevated app. Start StockFW in the background when I sign in registers a scheduled task for the account signed in at this desktop; it runs StockFW in the notification area without administrator rights, so it shows the firewall’s state and hands over to the elevated copy when you start one. You can turn it on or off later from the tray menu or Preferences › General. FinishThe installer can start StockFW for you. The first start is a good moment to open the elevated copy once, so monitoring gets switched on (below). What gets installed# WhereWhat C:\\Program Files\\StockFW\\StockFW.exeThe application. Its manifest says asInvoker: it runs with the rights of whoever starts it and never raises them itself. …\\StockFW\\*.dll, lib\\, share\\, etc\\The GTK 3 runtime, complete, so the app does not depend on anything else on the machine. …\\StockFW\\StockFW-elevated.vbs, .cmd, .ps1The elevated launchers. Each finds StockFW.exe next to itself and asks Windows for administrator rights with a normal UAC prompt. …\\StockFW\\README.md, CHANGELOG.md, LICENSE.txtThe project README, the changelog, and the licence summary with both texts. Task Scheduler › \\StockFWThe start-at-sign-in task, if you chose it: one per user, without administrator rights. Start menu › StockFWStockFW, StockFW (Elevated), Documentation, Uninstall. Registry, HKLM\\Software\\StockFWThe install folder, and the usual entry under Apps & features. Two ways to run it# Normal: the “StockFW” shortcut# Runs with your own rights. You can watch events (once monitoring is enabled), browse and edit the policy, run the simulator, inspect and attest programs, and change preferences. You cannot change the firewall: pressing Apply, Start or a profile switch fails with a clear message, “Administrator privileges required”, and the change stays saved for later. Nothing is half-applied. Elevated: the “StockFW (Elevated)” shortcut# Shows a UAC prompt, then runs with administrator rights. Apply, Start and Stop, the profile switches and Sentinel’s auto-block all work. Windows itself protects this window: a program running with ordinary rights cannot send it clicks or keystrokes. How the launcher stays safe The launcher resolves every path relative to its own folder, never from %TEMP%, your profile or the registry, and it refuses to start if the GTK runtime is not beside the executable instead of searching for one elsewhere. An earlier version added a build folder to PATH; that was removed, because a user-writable folder on an administrator’s search path is how a planted DLL gets loaded. Verified test-installer-spawn.sh Enable monitoring# Windows writes a firewall event for every connection only when the audit policy asks it to. On its first elevated run StockFW enables the Filtering Platform Connection subcategory with auditpol; running it again is harmless. After that, events 5156 (allowed) and 5157 (blocked) appear in the Security log and on the Events page. If the Events page says monitoring is not enabled, open the elevated app once, or run this in an administrator PowerShell: PowerShellCopyPS> auditpol /set /subcategory:\"Filtering Platform Connection\" /success:enable /failure:enable This is the one machine setting StockFW changes that is not a firewall rule, and the app says so when it does it. Where StockFW keeps its files# Everything is yours to read, copy or delete. Nothing is hidden in a database. FileHolds %LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\inbound\\…, outbound\\…The policy, one file per rule kind. See Files and formats. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\applied.csvThe last policy the firewall accepted: the journal Apply measures against and rolls back to. %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\stockfw.confPreferences. %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\audit.logOne line per apply, attest, forget and Sentinel action. Append-only. %APPDATA%\\stockfw\\trust.csv, behavior.csv, autoblocksThe Trust baseline, the Copilot’s learned behaviour, and Sentinel’s staged blocks. %LOCALAPPDATA%\\stockfw\\events\\The event history, when Keep event history is on. Daily files only your account can read. %ProgramData%\\StockFW\\intel\\The threat-intel configuration and downloaded lists, writable by administrators only. Rules StockFW creates in Windows Firewall are named StockFW-… (policy rules) and StockFW … (Preferences rules), and the blocklists live in the rule group StockFW Threat Intel, so you can find them all in wf.msc. Update# Run the new installer over the old one. It offers to uninstall the previous version first and waits for that to finish, asking a running StockFW to quit cleanly on the way. Your policy, preferences, history and baselines are in your profile, not in the install folder, so they are kept. Uninstall# Settings › Apps, or Uninstall in the Start menu folder. The uninstaller asks a running StockFW to quit, removes every user’s start-at-sign-in task, then removes the program, the runtime and the shortcuts. It does not remove the firewall rules StockFW created or your files in %APPDATA% and %LOCALAPPDATA%; press Stop firewall in the app before uninstalling if you want the rules gone, or delete the StockFW-… rules in wf.msc. Build it yourself# StockFW builds on Windows with MSYS2 in about five commands. See Build on Windows."},{"t":"IPv6","u":"docs/ipv6.html","s":"Protection","h":[["Windows","windows"],["Linux","linux"],["Which family a rule reaches","which-family-a-rule-reaches"],["What IPv6 always needs","what-ipv6-always-needs"],["Router mode","router-mode"],["What the app tells you","what-the-app-tells-you"],["Testing it without IPv6","testing-it-without-ipv6"]],"x":"IPv6 A rule that only exists for IPv4 is a door left open on every network that also speaks IPv6, which today is most of them. StockFW tells you, per rule and on the Overview, exactly which families are filtered, and on Linux it filters both from the same policy. Windows# Windows Windows Firewall rules apply to both families unless an address scopes them, so a rule for Anyone covers IPv4 and IPv6, and a rule for 2001:db8::5 covers IPv6 only. The Overview’s two tiles both read “Filtered” whenever the firewall is on. One limit: the Windows engine takes addresses, not names, so a rule whose target is a host name covers nothing there, and the Covers column says so. Known gap Linux# Linux The whole policy is rendered before the kernel is touched into two payloads, one for iptables-restore and one for ip6tables-restore, each with a filter part and, in router mode, a nat part. The backend snapshots StockFW’s chains in both families, loads IPv4, then IPv6, and on any failure puts back every table it changed. Each payload is loaded with --noflush and declares only StockFW’s own chains, so rules that Docker, libvirt or ufw keep in the same tables are never seen, and the hook from INPUT into INBOUND is not removed and re-added during a re-apply. Verified test-apply-dual.sh The rollback covers both families, always. The first version rolled IPv6 back and skipped IPv4 whenever the IPv6 restore itself failed, which would have left IPv4 on the new policy while the dialog said nothing changed; four “teeth” runs now pin it, each removing one part of the rollback and watching a specific check go red. Which family a rule reaches# TargetRendered into An IPv4 address or networkThe IPv4 payload only. An IPv6 address or networkThe IPv6 payload only. Anyone, or emptyBoth. A host nameLooked up once per apply and written as each family’s addresses, so no name is left for the tools to resolve while loading. A name with no address in one family gets a note. An allow whose name cannot be looked up is left out and reported (it fails closed). A block whose name cannot be looked up refuses the apply while rules are loaded, so the last good addresses stay blocked; when nothing is loaded yet (Start), it is left out and the notes begin with “NOT BLOCKED:”, because refusing would leave every host unfiltered over one name. Verified test_restore_plan A port forwardIts destination’s family, written as [2001:db8::5]:80 for IPv6. Masquerading stays IPv4-only. A destination that is a name or a range is left out and noted. The same decision drives the Covers column on the Policy page, stockfw policy list and its JSON, and a test checks that “Covers” names a family exactly when that family’s payload contains the rule. Verified test-core.sh What IPv6 always needs# Neighbour discovery is ICMPv6, not ARP, so it goes through the filter, and a firewall that drops it takes the host off the network. Ahead of every preference and never rate-limited, INBOUND accepts packet-too-big, time-exceeded and parameter-problem; router advertisements from fe80::/10, neighbour solicitations and advertisements, all with hop limit 255; multicast listener discovery with hop limit 1; and DHCPv6 replies from link-local. OUTBOUND lets the host’s own solicitations, advertisements, listener reports, packet-too-big and DHCPv6 requests out, ahead of restrictive mode’s default drop. The ICMP preferences map to ICMPv6 where a counterpart exists; timestamps, address masks and source quench are IPv4-only and labelled so. Router mode# IPv6 forwarding is hooked only in router mode, because container and VM runtimes accept forwarded traffic in their own tables and would otherwise be cut by INBOUND’s default drop. An IPv6 forward needs net.ipv6.conf.all.forwarding=1, and a forwarding host ignores router advertisements, which would drop its own default route; before turning forwarding on, the backend sets accept_ra=2 on the external interface, and only if it was 1, since 0 means someone turned advertisements off on purpose. What the app tells you# The Overview has an IPv6 traffic tile that reads the kernel: “Filtered”, or “Not filtered” with the reason in the sidebar: ip6tables not installed, chains not loaded, loaded but not hooked, or the firewall stopped. “None on this system” when the kernel has no IPv6 stack. It never infers IPv6 from IPv4. stockfw status has an IPv6 line and an ipv6 object in its JSON. The dashboard’s Firewall tile reads “In force for IPv4 and IPv6” or warns “IPv4 only”. A successful apply on a host without ip6tables is a success with a note, not a failure: “IPv6 is not filtered: ip6tables is not installed”. Install the package and Apply again. Testing it without IPv6# The project’s test container has no IPv6 stack. STOCKFW_ASSUME_IPV6=1 makes the backend load the IPv6 payload anyway, so rule loading is exercised for real in a private network namespace while packet behaviour is checked as rule text. The screenshots on this site were taken that way."},{"t":"Overview","u":"docs/overview.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["The protection card","the-protection-card"],["The counters","the-counters"],["Open connections","open-connections"],["Network adapters","network-adapters"],["Recent blocks","recent-blocks"],["Lock policy","lock-policy"],["The sidebar","the-sidebar"]],"x":"Overview The first page answers one question, “is this computer protected?”, in one sentence, and then shows the four things you would check next. Protected, both families filtered, ten rules in force, one virtual adapter hidden and counted, and the last blocks with their source and time. The protection card# Green with a shield when the firewall is running and your policy is in force; red when it is stopped. The sentence under the heading says what that means for traffic, and the four tiles break it down: IPv4 traffic, IPv6 traffic “Filtered” or “Not filtered”, separately. On Linux the IPv6 tile reads the real state of the kernel: if ip6tables is not installed, the IPv6 chains are not loaded, or they are loaded but not hooked, it says “Not filtered” and the sidebar shows the reason, rather than inferring IPv6 from IPv4. On Windows both read “Filtered” when the firewall is on, because Windows Firewall rules cover both families. Policy How many rules are saved. Saved is not the same as applied; the Policy page says which rules are live. Engine What is doing the filtering: Linux (iptables) or Windows Firewall. On Windows the card also shows the profile in force (Domain, Private or Public) and the network the computer is on, read through the firewall’s own interfaces. Start firewall puts your saved policy in force: on Linux it loads the rules, on Windows it switches the firewall profiles on. Stop firewall takes StockFW’s rules out again (Linux) or switches the profiles off (Windows), and the card turns red, because that is what it means. View events jumps to the Events page. On Linux, Start firewall and the start on program startup preference read the saved policy first and go through the same gate as Apply, so the firewall never briefly runs an empty policy. The counters# Four tiles count since the app opened: inbound events (with how many were blocked), outbound events, open connections (with how many programs own them), and traffic right now across your adapters. “Idle” means no bytes in the last second. These are the same numbers the Events and Activity pages show in detail. Open connections# Every established TCP connection, with the program that owns it, the remote address, the port, the service name and the direction. On Linux the program is found through /proc/<pid>/fd; on Windows through the TCP table. When the owner cannot be read the cell shows ?, never a guess. Direction is decided by what is listening: a connection on a port that something on this computer listens on was accepted, so it is Inbound and named by that port. Only connections nobody listens for fall back to guessing from port numbers, which is what older versions did for everything and got wrong whenever both ends were high ports. Verified test-core.sh Connections between two programs on this computer are hidden by default and counted at the bottom of the table (“2 local connections are hidden · Show them”), and so are the dashboard’s own sockets, which exist only because someone is looking. Network adapters# Each adapter with its type (Ethernet, Wireless, Virtual), address, totals and current rate. Virtual adapters, which is what Docker bridges, VM networks and VPN tunnels create, are hidden by default and counted, with Show them to list them. On Linux the type comes from sysfs: a wireless extension means Wireless, a backing bus device means Ethernet, anything else is Virtual. Recent blocks# The last few blocked connections, each with the remote address, what blocked it (your policy, or Sentinel) and the time. All events opens the Events page. When nothing has been blocked since the app opened, the card says so instead of showing an empty list. Lock policy# The button in the top corner. While the policy is locked, every Apply, from any page or from the command line, becomes a dry run: the change is measured and saved, an audit line reads “policy held, firewall untouched”, and the engine is left exactly as it is. Use it when you are editing a policy you do not want to apply piecemeal. Audit-only mode on the Policy page does the same thing from the other side. Verified test_apply_gate The sidebar# The sidebar lists the pages: Overview, Events, Activity, Policy, Threat Intel, Copilot and Trust. Its bottom repeats the verdict, Protected or Not protected, with the engine’s name, so it is visible from every page. Preferences, Help and About StockFW sit above it; F1 opens the help at the topic for the page you are on."},{"t":"Policy","u":"docs/policy.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["How to think about it","how-to-think-about-it"],["The rule kinds","the-rule-kinds"],["Covers","covers"],["One direction or both","one-direction-or-both"],["Apply, step by step","apply-step-by-step"],["Audit-only and Lock","audit-only-and-lock"],["What is checked before anything is saved","what-is-checked-before-anything-is-saved"],["In the engine","in-the-engine"]],"x":"Policy Your rules, grouped by kind, each one a row you can read. Saving a rule changes a file in your profile; pressing Apply changes the firewall, after showing you what will change. Rules are grouped by kind. Point at a Covers cell and the tooltip says why a rule reaches the families it does. How to think about it# The engine’s defaults do most of the work: new inbound connections are denied unless a rule allows them, new outbound connections are allowed unless a rule denies them, and replies to connections that are already open always pass. StockFW rules are exceptions to those defaults, which is why the kinds are named the way they are: allow inbound, deny outbound. There is no inbound-deny kind because inbound is already denied; to stop trusting a host you remove its allow. The rule kinds# On the pageCommand-line nameFieldsMeaning Allow connections from hostinbound-allow-fromhostEverything from this address, network or host name may come in. Allow inbound serviceinbound-allow-serviceservice, port, forThis port may be reached from for: a host, a network such as 192.168.1.0/24, or Anyone. Forward serviceinbound-forwardservice, firewall port, to, portTraffic arriving on the firewall port is sent to another host and port. Router mode only; needs an external interface in Preferences, and the destination must be an address. Allow connections to hostoutbound-allow-tohostEverything to this host may go out, even in restrictive outbound mode. Allow outbound serviceoutbound-allow-serviceservice, port, forThis port may be reached outbound, for the given host or anyone. Deny connections to hostoutbound-deny-tohostNothing may go out to this host. Deny outbound serviceoutbound-deny-serviceservice, port, forThis port may not be reached outbound, for the given host or anyone. Allow / deny connections from (router mode)outbound-allow-from, outbound-deny-fromhostFor a LAN host behind this machine: what it may or may not send out through it. Every rule can carry a comment, which is kept in the file and shown in its own column. The “service” field is a name for your benefit; the port is what the engine sees. Common names (SSH, HTTP, HTTPS, DNS, RDP and so on) are known, so the simulator and the Events page can use them. Covers# A rule reaches the address families its target can reach, and the Covers column says which, with the reason as a tooltip. TargetCovers An IPv4 address or networkIPv4 only An IPv6 address or networkIPv6 only Anyone, or an empty fieldIPv4 + IPv6 A host nameIPv4 + IPv6 on Linux, where the name is looked up once at apply and written as each family’s addresses. Nothing on Windows, whose firewall takes addresses only. A forwardIts destination’s family. Nothing when the destination is a name or a range, which the generator leaves out. The same answer appears as [IPv4 only] in stockfw policy list and as covers and covers_why in its JSON, and a test checks that, for each kind of target, “Covers” names a family exactly when that family’s generated rules contain the rule. Verified test-core.sh One direction or both# Inbound and outbound are separate rule spaces, so allowing a peer both ways would normally take two rules. On the groups where a rule has an opposite-direction twin, the Add row carries a Direction control: Inbound only (or Outbound only) by default, or Bi-directional, which stages the twin as part of the same change. The pairs are exact: allow-from pairs with allow-to, and inbound allow-service pairs with outbound allow-service. Deny kinds and forwards have no twin, so the control is absent there rather than disabled. The pair is saved as one change: if the write fails, both rules come back out. Verified test_direction_pairing This is about new connections in the other direction. Replies to an open connection never need a rule; the engine is stateful. Apply, step by step# Save first. Adding, editing or removing a rule writes the policy file immediately, and if the write fails the change is taken back out of memory, so what you see is always what is on disk. Measure. Pressing Apply policy compares the saved policy with the journal of what the engine last accepted (applied.csv) and shows the counts: “+1 added (1 allow, 0 block), −0 removed, =8 unchanged”. Nothing has happened yet; you can say No. Push. On Linux the whole policy is rendered into an iptables-restore payload and an ip6tables-restore payload and loaded with --noflush, so only StockFW’s own chains are replaced, each family in one atomic step; if the IPv6 load is refused, the IPv4 load is undone. On Windows each rule is one netsh advfirewall command, and only the rules that changed are touched. Preferences that compile to rules (ICMP, broadcasts, reserved sources) are pushed in the same step. Record. On success the journal is updated to what is now live, so the next Apply measures against reality. Report. The dialog says what the engine said. On Linux a successful apply can carry notes (“ip6tables is not installed”, “the forward to 192.168.1.30 was left out: no external interface is set”); on Windows a rule the engine refused makes the apply partial, and the gate restores the last good policy and reports whether the restore worked. Audit. One line in audit.log: the source (Policy page, Events page, Copilot, Sentinel or cli), the mode, the counts, and any notes. Verified test_apply_gate Apply policy changes immediately, on the Interface › Policy preference page, skips the button: each edit is pushed as soon as it is saved, through the same gate and with the same audit line. Audit-only and Lock# Audit-only on this page and Lock policy on the Overview both hold the firewall still. While either is on, Apply measures and records the change, writes an audit line reading “policy held, firewall untouched”, and leaves the engine alone. Turn it off and press Apply when you are ready. Rehearsing a policy this way is the intended use. Verified test_apply_gate What is checked before anything is saved# Ports are digits only (so 80abc and 0x50 are refused), 0 to 65535, and a range’s first port is not above its last. Hosts are an IPv4 or IPv6 address, a network in CIDR form (192.168.1.0/24, 2001:db8::/32), or a host name; a host name may not start with a hyphen. A comma or line break inside a field is refused, because the files are plain comma-separated text and it would become a second field. Verified test_policy_load_validation_f1 When a policy file is loaded and a row does not parse, that row is skipped and counted (“2 malformed policy rules were skipped”), never guessed at or rewritten, and saving does not silently drop or change it. Verified test-core.sh In the engine# On Windows every rule StockFW creates is named StockFW-…, with the same canonical name used to add and to delete it, so a change never leaves a stale copy behind; a verified clear re-enumerates and retries until none remain. On Linux the rules live in chains StockFW owns (INBOUND, OUTBOUND, STOCKFW-DNAT, STOCKFW-SNAT) and nothing else in the tables is touched. In both cases wf.msc or nft list ruleset can confirm what StockFW says. The files behind the page are described in Files and formats."},{"t":"Preferences","u":"docs/preferences.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["How the window works","how-the-window-works"],["StockFW › General","stockfw-general"],["StockFW › Events","stockfw-events"],["StockFW › Policy","stockfw-policy"],["Firewall › Firewall","firewall-firewall"],["Firewall › Network","firewall-network"],["Firewall › ICMP","firewall-icmp"],["Firewall › Protection","firewall-protection"],["How firewall settings reach the engine","apply"],["Where they are stored","storage"]],"x":"Preferences Open it from the bottom of the sidebar or with Ctrl+,. The window has a dark sidebar with two groups, StockFW for how the program behaves and Firewall for what the engine does, and each page opens with a line on what it is for. A row that cannot work on your platform is greyed with the reason, and a page the platform cannot act on is not offered at all, so you never flip a switch that does nothing. How the window works# The General page on Linux. On Windows the sidebar has no Firewall or Network page. Changes are marked where they are. A row you changed gets a bar on its left, its page gets a dot in the sidebar, and the footer counts what Save will write. Cancel writes nothing. Help in the footer, or F1, opens the Help window at the topic for the page you are on. Help stays open when Preferences closes. The settings live in one text file, stockfw.conf. See where they are stored. Verified ui-prefs-smoke The grouped sidebar, the page heads, the centred column and the change marks are checked on both platforms. StockFW › General# Show a tray icon On by default. Puts StockFW in the notification area, with a menu to open the window, pause protection, reach every page or quit. Close and minimize to the tray On by default. Closing or minimising the window hides it in the tray instead of quitting, so event monitoring and Sentinel keep running. It needs the tray icon; where the desktop has no tray area, closing quits. See Background, tray and sign-in. Start the firewall when StockFW opens Off by default. Turns the firewall on each time StockFW starts, through the same gate as the Start button, so an empty policy is never applied on the way. Off, the firewall is left as it was. Start StockFW in the background when I sign in Off by default. Puts StockFW in the notification area when you sign in to the desktop. On Windows it registers a per-user scheduled task that runs without administrator rights, so the sign-in copy only shows the state; start StockFW (Elevated) for monitoring and changes and it takes over. On Linux it writes an autostart entry that runs without root, so that copy shows the firewall's state as unknown, never as Off. The note under the switch says whether your firewall rules depend on this: on Windows they never do, and on Linux it reads the state of the boot service (enabled, installed but not enabled, or missing). The switch is greyed with the reason where this copy cannot change it. Verified test-autostart.sh StockFW › Events# What the list shows, how much it keeps, and whether events are saved to disk. Hide repeated events Off by default. Leaves out a hit that repeats the one just before it, so a scan does not flood the list. The Copilot still sees every event. Skip events not addressed to this computer On by default. Leaves out broadcasts and traffic meant for other machines. Events kept in the list 100,000 by default, up to 1,000,000. Past this the oldest go. Each event costs about 75 bytes of memory, so the default is a few megabytes. Keep event history Off by default. Saves every listed event to daily files only your account can read, so a restart does not empty the Events page. Keep history for (days), 30 by default, deletes older files; History size limit (MB), 200 by default, deletes the oldest files past that size whatever their age. The Saved history row says how much is on disk and Delete history removes it after asking. See History on disk. Verified test-eventstore.sh StockFW › Policy# When changes to your rules reach the firewall. The audit-only mode is shown here but changed on the Policy page. Apply policy changes immediately Each rule you add, edit or remove on the Policy page goes to the firewall as soon as it is saved, through the same gate and with the same audit line as the Apply button. Off, every change waits for Apply policy, and the Policy page shows what is staged. Audit-only testing mode Shown read-only with its state, On or Off. On, policy changes are saved and written to the audit log but the firewall is not changed. It is a safety control, so it is switched on the Policy page, where the Lock on the Overview page is also explained; the button here takes you there. Firewall › Firewall# Linux This page is offered on Linux only. Windows Firewall always drops silently, leaves the outbound default to its network profiles and logs through its own audit policy, so there is nothing here it could act on. How blocked connections are answered, and what may leave this computer. Answer to a blocked connection Drop silently: the sender hears nothing, as if this computer were not there. Reject with an error: the sender is told at once that the connection was refused. Connections from this computer Allow all outgoing, the default, or Block outgoing unless allowed. In the restrictive mode only connections that an outbound allow rule on the Policy page names can leave this computer; everything else is blocked, including software updates, until you allow it, and the page says so in a warning when you pick it. Your outbound deny rules apply either way. Log allowed connections On by default. Records the first packet of each new connection your policy allows, at most ten a second, so Events, Activity and the Copilot see allowed traffic as well as blocked. Off, the Copilot's login and beacon checks see only refused traffic. Firewall › Network# Linux Offered on Linux only. Windows has no equivalent that the app can drive honestly, so the page is not shown there rather than shown inert. The pickers list what is really attached. eth0 here is the staged network’s only adapter. Internet device The adapter that connects this computer to the Internet. Needed for port forwards and for the blocks on the Protection page. A device marked not present is not connected right now. Local network device The adapter that faces your own network. Only used for connection sharing; traffic arriving on it is accepted. Share the Internet connection (NAT) Lets devices on your local network reach the Internet through this computer, IPv4 only. It needs both devices chosen. Port forwards need this. Firewall › ICMP# Each type has one line saying what it lets in. The note under the master switch says what IPv6 always allows. Filter ICMP messages turns the page on. Off, every ICMP message is let in. On, only the types switched on below may arrive: TypeDefaultWhat it lets in Echo request (ping)onOther computers may ping this one. Linux rate-limits it to one a second. Echo replyonEcho replies this computer did not ask for. Replies to your own pings get through either way. Host unreachableon“Host unreachable” reports not about a connection this computer made. Reports about your own connections get through either way. TracerouteonUDP port 33434, where Unix-style traceroute probes arrive. Not ICMP at all, kept here because the original kept it here. Windows tracerouteonEvery “destination unreachable” report, which Windows tracert needs. Traceroute and tracert from this computer do not need it. RedirectionoffRouters may send traffic another way. Seldom needed, and open to abuse. Timestamping, Address masking, Source quenchingoffIPv4 only, and the labels say so. Obsolete; timestamping also gives away the clock. Linux IPv6 uses the same choices. Neighbour discovery, router advertisements, “packet too big” and DHCPv6 replies are always allowed, because IPv6 stops working without them; time-exceeded and parameter-problem are always allowed on IPv4 for the same reason. The page says so under the switch. Windows The choices become allow rules beside Windows' own, for IPv4 only; Windows' own rules may still allow some types you switch off, ICMPv6 is left to Windows, and nothing is rate-limited. The page carries both notes. Verified test-core.sh Firewall › Protection# Extra blocks for traffic that should never arrive. The two broadcast switches exist on Linux only. Block broadcasts from the Internet side, from the local network Linux Drop broadcast packets arriving "},{"t":"Security model","u":"docs/security.html","s":"Protection","h":[["What it is, in one paragraph","what-it-is-in-one-paragraph"],["Three boundaries","three-boundaries"],["One gate","one-gate"],["Why a program running as you cannot change the firewall","why-a-program-running-as-you-cannot-change-the-firewall"],["What tampering with the files buys","what-tampering-with-the-files-buys"],["The Copilot cannot be talked into anything","the-copilot-cannot-be-talked-into-anything"],["What leaves your computer","what-leaves-your-computer"],["The local dashboard","the-local-dashboard"],["Honest limits","honest-limits"]],"x":"Security model StockFW is a privileged program by nature: when it is allowed to, it rewrites the firewall. This page explains the boundaries it keeps, so you can judge for yourself whether running it makes your computer safer. Every claim here is spelled out with its evidence in the architecture and security document, chapter 5 and the release addenda. What it is, in one paragraph# StockFW contains no packet filter, no driver and no service. It configures the engine your operating system already has, through the tools that system documents (netsh advfirewall and the firewall COM interfaces on Windows; iptables-restore, ip6tables-restore and nft on Linux), and it reads the verdicts that engine already logs. If StockFW is closed, crashes or is uninstalled, the engine keeps enforcing whatever was last applied. Nothing StockFW does can make the firewall less enforced by failing. Three boundaries# Enforcement Anything that changes firewall state must run with administrator or root rights, and StockFW contains no code that raises its own rights: no runas, no ShellExecute, no sudo, no setuid helper, no polkit rule. The Windows manifest says asInvoker. Elevation happens only when you choose the elevated shortcut and answer the UAC prompt, or type sudo. Verified test-installer-spawn.sh Input Text from the system (event logs), from disk (policy files, preferences, journals) and from you (the policy editor, the simulator) is untrusted until it has been parsed into typed values. Ports are digits only; addresses are parsed with inet_pton and never resolved by the engine; every generated Linux rule line passes a character whitelist before it reaches a restore payload; every Windows argument is one argv token quoted by the exact inverse of the child’s parser. There is no shell anywhere: not system(), not cmd /c, not sh -c. Verified test_restore_plan Display What the app shows is derived data. An event is text in a list, never markup and never a command, so a hostile program cannot make the Events page do something by choosing what to connect to. One gate# Every path that can change the firewall, the Apply button, an event’s right-click menu, a Copilot suggestion, Sentinel’s auto-block, the Preferences dialog and stockfw apply, calls one function. It measures the change against the journal of what is live, honours Audit-only and Lock, touches only the rules that changed, rolls back to the journal if the engine refuses and verifies the rollback instead of assuming it, and appends one line to the audit log. There is no second door, and the gate is tested with a scripted fake backend through every branch. Verified test_apply_gate Why a program running as you cannot change the firewall# The interesting attacker is not someone on the network, who can only generate traffic for StockFW to display. It is malware already running with your rights, trying to make the elevated StockFW install its rules. The walk-through from the architecture document: It rewrites the policy files with permissive rows. Nothing happens: there is no file watcher, and the files are read when the app loads them. The rows are visible on the Policy page. It presses Apply in the unprivileged window. The engine refuses, the app says “Administrator privileges required”, and the rows never reached a privileged process. It sends clicks to the elevated window. Windows’ UIPI drops input from a lower-integrity process. It plants a fake netsh.exe or iptables on the search path. On Windows every tool is started by its full path in the system folder (netsh, wevtutil, auditpol, curl), never from the program’s own folder or the current one, and DLLs load only from StockFW’s folder and System32. On Linux a root StockFW runs iptables, ip6tables, nft, journalctl and curl only from /usr/sbin, /usr/bin, /sbin or /bin, whatever PATH says, so a script planted in ~/.local/bin under sudo -E never runs. The installed program sits in a folder only administrators can write. Verified test-core.sh It races the temporary files the app uses to capture tool output. They are created atomically and reopened share-read; a planted file causes a sharing violation and the error path. Each step is a structural dead end, not a detection. The same reasoning holds on Linux with root in place of administrator, where additionally the installer never widens sudo’s search path or installs any helper. Verified test-installer-spawn.sh What tampering with the files buys# The policy and preferences are your files, in your profile, writable by you. That is a deliberate choice (back up by copying, audit with diff), and its consequence is stated plainly: file integrity is not a security boundary in StockFW; the Apply button is. If someone…Then… rewrites the policy while StockFW is closednothing changes until you open the app, see the rows, and press Apply with privileges. rewrites it while StockFW is openthe in-memory policy wins on the next save; a later load counts malformed rows; Apply still needs the button. deletes a filethe app reports it; the engine keeps its rules. replaces it with garbagerows that do not parse are skipped and counted, never half-applied. edits the journala row that cannot be trusted fails the journal load, and the apply refuses rather than roll back to a lie. Verified test_apply_gate The Copilot cannot be talked into anything# There is no language model and no prompt. The what-if parser recognises a dozen words; the behaviour analysis is counters. An attacker’s only input is the traffic it generates, which becomes rows and numbers, never instructions. Everything the Copilot proposes is printed as the exact rule in a Yes/No dialog, allow rules are never applied without that dialog, and the one autonomous action, Sentinel’s auto-block, can only add a deny for a public destination, expires in ten minutes, and is fenced as described on the Copilot page. Verified test-adversarial.sh What leaves your computer# Nothing on its own. StockFW has no telemetry, no analytics, no update check and no account. The only processes it starts are local system tools. Event data lives in memory and on screen, and on disk only if you turn Keep event history on; the policy records intent, not history; the audit log records what StockFW did, not what you connected to. What it downloads, each only after you turn it on and accept: threat-intel feeds, from their publishers and no mirror, and IPFire’s location database for country blocking; the consent dialog says what the publisher learns (your public address and StockFW/version). Beyond that, a Linux rule that names a host looks the name up in DNS when it is applied, and a link you click in Help opens in your browser. The Your data topic in the app’s Help says the same. One machine setting outside the firewall is changed, and disclosed when it happens: on Windows, enabling monitoring turns on the Filtering Platform Connection audit subcategory with auditpol. The local dashboard# stockfw dashboard is the one place StockFW listens on a socket. It binds 127.0.0.1 only and has no option to bind anything else; before a byte is read it asks the kernel who owns the connecting socket and closes anything that is not your own account on 127.0.0.1 (never root, never another user); the link it prints holds a 128-bit code that works once, for two minutes, which the page swaps for a 256-bit session key sent in a header and compared in constant time; a link used twice ends every session; a request whose Host header is not exactly 127.0.0.1:port is refused, which closes DNS rebinding (localhost is refused too, since another account could listen on [::1]); the one POST needs the page’s own Origin and Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin; and the API is read-only, so even with a session a page cannot change the firewall. The code never appears on a command line: the link is printed only to a terminal, a pipe gets the path of a private launch file (mode 0600, in a fresh private directory) instead, the browser is handed that fil"},{"t":"What-if simulator","u":"docs/simulator.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Where it lives","where-it-lives"],["How to phrase a question","how-to-phrase-a-question"],["How a verdict is reached","how-a-verdict-is-reached"],["The window","the-window"],["What you can do","what-you-can-do"],["From a script","from-a-script"],["What it does not know","what-it-does-not-know"]],"x":"What-if simulator Type a sentence about a connection and StockFW tells you whether your firewall would allow it or block it, which rule decides, every rule it checked on the way, and what you can do about it. It is a question, not a change: asking writes nothing. Allowed by a rule. The rule’s name is the one the Policy page and the engine use, and “Why” lists every rule that was checked before it. Where it lives# At the bottom of the Copilot page, in the dashboard, and as stockfw simulate on the command line. All three run the same engine against the same policy files and the same firewall preferences, and the command-line tests assert that they agree. Verified test-cli.sh On the Copilot page the card keeps what you typed, so a question is refined rather than retyped. Under the entry, Try offers three example questions, and Read as shows, as chips, how your line was understood: the direction, the protocol, the port or service, the host, and, dashed, anything the line left open. The answer is one row: the verdict badge, one sentence, and Details, which opens the What-If window beside the page. The window is not modal, and asking again refreshes it in place. The last five questions stay listed, each one click from being asked again. The card on the Copilot page. Here the saved policy is not the one in force, because applies are on hold, and the answer says so before the verdict. How to phrase a question# Write it the way you would say it. The parser looks for the pieces below and ignores filler such as please, I want or can you; the order of words does not matter. Everything it recognised is shown back to you as Read as, so what gets evaluated is always what you typed, never a guess. Verified test-whatif.c You writeIt means inbound, incoming, ingress; outbound, outgoing, egressThe direction. Without one, the question is answered both ways, one tab per direction. from 192.168.1.50, to 8.8.8.8, reach, access, an address, a network such as 192.168.1.0/24, or [2001:db8::5]:443The remote end. internet, outside, anyone or anywhere means any host. Without a host, the host is left open. port 22, 22 port, :443, or a service name: SSH, RDP, HTTPS, DNS, TelnetThe port. A service name is looked up to its port and protocol (“SSH · port 22”); a number wins over a name when both appear. udp, icmp, pingThe protocol. A named service brings its own; a bare port is TCP; ping is ICMP echo request. allow, permit, open, let; block, deny, drop, rejectWhat you want. The answer still says what the firewall does now; the verb decides which fix is offered. “Is … allowed?” and “Can … reach …?” are plain questions. Sentences that workCopyCan 192.168.1.50 reach my SSH? Is outbound DNS to 8.8.8.8 allowed? block RDP from the internet allow tcp port 8443 inbound outbound tcp port 443 ping from 10.0.0.9 Whatever a line leaves open stays open. “outbound tcp port 443” names no host, so it is answered for every host your rules distinguish, and when they disagree the verdict is DEPENDS with the split spelled out. A line with nothing recognisable in it is an error, not an “outbound to anywhere” guess: the window says “StockFW could not read this question” and shows how to ask. How a verdict is reached# The line is parsed into a question: direction, protocol, port, host, your verb, and what was left open. Everything else that changes the answer is gathered: the Firewall preferences (ICMP types, the reject method, restrictive outbound, interfaces and NAT, the broadcast and reserved-source blocks), whether the firewall is running, whether the saved policy is the one actually loaded, Sentinel’s auto-blocks with their expiry, and on Windows the network profiles in force with their default actions. The policy is evaluated by a model of your firewall. Linux The INBOUND and OUTBOUND chains exactly as StockFW writes them, in order; the first rule that matches decides. The model renders itself as iptables lines and the test suite compares those with the real generator’s output, so the two cannot drift apart. Verified test-whatif.c Windows The policy as Windows Firewall rules plus the profile-scoped preference rules; any matching block wins over any allow, and with neither the profile’s default action decides. For each field the line left open, a representative of every range a rule draws on that field is tried. If they all agree, that is the answer. If not, the answer is DEPENDS: “Blocked for most hosts; allowed from 10.0.0.0/8 by rule ‘…’”. The answer carries the whole trace (every rule checked, skipped or matched), the deciding step, one plain sentence, and the fixes. A fix that would change the policy is offered only after the engine has re-run the question with the new row in place and found exactly the verdict you asked for, with nothing else changed. The policy it reads is the saved one, so you can rehearse a change before Apply: add a rule, ask, and only then push. When the saved policy is not what the firewall is running, the answer says so first (“Saved policy, applies on hold”), and when the firewall is stopped it says that nothing is filtered right now and what the verdict would be once it runs. Verified ui-simviz-smoke Blocked by the default. The fix was checked before it was offered, and the exact policy row it would write is printed under it. The window# The window is one scrolling page of cards. The verdict. A badge reading ALLOWED, BLOCKED or DEPENDS, the connection in one sentence (“Inbound TCP port 22 (SSH) from 192.168.1.50 to this computer”), what you typed, and the answer in one line that names the deciding rule. When the answer rests on something that is not the live firewall, an amber strip says so. Both ways. A line asked without a direction gets one tab per direction, each with its own verdict. The diagram. Where the traffic starts, the StockFW wall, where it is going. Each card says what it is (“This computer”, “LAN host 192.168.1.50”, “Internet host”, “Any host”) and which network it sits on; the far card names the port. The packet plays the verdict: it reaches the wall, and then passes through to a glowing destination, or bounces back off a red wall as the far line turns grey, or waits at an amber wall under a question mark when the answer depends. The round button replays it. With animations off, the final frame is drawn at once. Verified ui-simviz-smoke What you can do. The fixes, described below. The connection. Direction, protocol, from, to, port or service, the firewall model (“Linux iptables: the first matching rule decides” or “Windows Firewall: any matching block wins”), whether the firewall is running, whether the saved policy is live, and on Windows the profile in force. Why. The trace as a numbered list. Rules that did not match are folded into one line (“Rules 1 to 10 did not match”) that opens on a click; the deciding step is highlighted and marked DECIDES; a rule from your policy carries an Open in Policy link to its row. For a DEPENDS answer the cases come first, each with its own badge and the rule that decides it. Good to know. Everything the answer had to assume, such as “No valid internet-facing interface is set, so the reserved-address and broadcast blocks are not in force.” The window does not block the app behind it; Esc or Close dismisses it, and asking another question refreshes it in place. It is drawn with no images, in a light and a dark palette picked from your theme. An answer that depends on what the line left open. Both cases are spelled out, and the one fix that resolves them is offered. What you can do# Every fix in this card was checked before it was offered, and each has one button. Create allow rule or Create block rule, when a single row gives you the verdict you asked for. The exact row is printed under the offer, as the Policy page would show it (“Rule to add (Allow inbound service): TCP, 8443, Anyone, What-If: allow TCP 8443”). A created allow always names one port and one protocol: never portless, never every port for anyone. Open"},{"t":"Threat-intel blocklists","u":"docs/threat-intel.html","s":"Protection","h":[["The feeds","the-feeds"],["Consent","consent"],["What a feed is checked for","what-a-feed-is-checked-for"],["What can never be blocked","what-can-never-be-blocked"],["How it reaches the engine","how-it-reaches-the-engine"],["What the kernel is really doing","what-the-kernel-is-really-doing"],["Where it shows up","where-it-shows-up"],["Files","files"]],"x":"Threat-intel blocklists A policy says what you allow. A blocklist says what the wider community has seen attacking, hosting botnet controllers or spraying brute-force logins, as address ranges that are refused on sight. StockFW brings well-known feeds in, off until you turn each one on, with the same discipline as the rest of the app: nothing silent, nothing that cannot be undone, and the kernel’s real state shown rather than the configuration. Every list is off until you turn it on and accept its terms. The state card says what the kernel is really doing, not what is configured. Linux On Linux the lists are one nftables table of StockFW’s own. Windows On Windows they are Windows Firewall rules in StockFW’s own rule group, StockFW Threat Intel. The engine, the store’s formats, the build of the rule set and every safety subtraction are the same code on both. Blocking by country or network owner is Linux only in this version. The design, with its review findings, is docs/design/1.0.7-threat-intel-design.md in the repository. The feeds# Every feed is off until you switch it on and accept its terms. A feed is fetched from its own publisher or not at all: no mirrors, no aggregator of StockFW’s own. Each entry carries the host it contacts, its information and terms pages, the licence where it has been verified (otherwise “See the provider’s terms”), whether it is for non-commercial use only, its format, its default direction and how often it updates. FeedWhat it listsDefault directionUpdates Spamhaus DROP, IPv4 and IPv6Networks hijacked or leased by professional spam and malware operations. Recommended.both12 h abuse.ch Feodo TrackerBotnet command-and-control servers. Recommended.both6 h DShieldThe top attacking networks seen by the Internet Storm Center. Non-commercial terms.in24 h FireHOL level 1An aggregate that already contains the three above and DShield. Turning it on next to them adds a note.in24 h blocklist.deAddresses reported by fail2ban installations.in24 h Emerging Threats compromised hostsHosts known to be compromised.in24 h CINS ArmyPoor-reputation addresses from the CINS sensor network.in24 h Tor exit relaysA policy choice, not a threat list; labelled as such.in6 h A custom feed is any URL that passes the URL rule, with a name, a format and a direction; typing the URL is your consent for that host. A local list is a CSV or plain file you import yourself, with no network involved. Direction means what it means for a stateful firewall: in stops them connecting to you, out stops you connecting to them, both does both. Replies to connections you opened are never affected by an in list. Consent# The first time you turn a feed on, a dialog says which host will be contacted and how often, what that host learns (your public address and the string StockFW/version), the licence and a link to the terms, and that lists contain mistakes and how to make an exception. Only after Accept does anything download; with nothing turned on StockFW makes no network request at all. Downloads use curl from the system folder, by absolute path, with size caps and timeouts; behind a proxy, set it on the page or with stockfw intel proxy (one with a user name or password is refused, because a command line is readable by every local user). While StockFW runs it checks hourly for feeds that are due; while it is closed nothing updates them except stockfw intel update from cron or a timer. A failed or refused update never removes the previous copy, and a copy that shrank to almost nothing, rolled back to an older date or jumped in coverage is refused until you accept it. What a feed is checked for# A downloaded file is text from the internet and is treated as hostile until it has passed every guard: a 64 MB size cap before it is read; strict parsing (addresses, CIDRs and a-b ranges, never names); no single IPv4 interval wider than a /8 and no IPv6 interval wider than a /16; no more than an eighth of the IPv4 space in one list; a list that is empty or mostly unparseable is refused and the set keeps what it had; and a sudden shrink is judged against the previous size. Content dates are read where a format has one, and a list older than seven update intervals is marked stale. Verified test-feed.c What can never be blocked# Before anything reaches the kernel, the engine subtracts from every set, and lists in its notes what it subtracted: loopback, link-local, multicast, the private ranges, carrier NAT, the reserved and documentation ranges, and the NAT64 prefixes; your own exceptions; this computer’s own addresses, its connected prefixes and its default gateways; the DNS servers in use, on Linux read from /etc/resolv.conf and, when those are loopback stubs, from systemd-resolved’s and NetworkManager’s real lists, on Windows from the adapters; the peer of your SSH or Remote Desktop session and every established inbound SSH or RDP connection, so a list cannot lock you out of the machine you are configuring it on; the feeds’ own hosts, so a list cannot block its own updates. Then, for sets that block outbound, each IPv4 interval is also added to the IPv6 set inside 64:ff9b::/96, so NAT64 does not bypass the list. Over all sets there are at most 300,000 intervals; above that the build fails and names the largest sets. How it reaches the engine# Linux One nftables table, inet stockfw_intel, owned by StockFW and replaced in a single nft -f - transaction, so it applies whole or not at all and a failed apply leaves the previous table in force. The script goes to nft on standard input through GSubprocess; no shell ever sees an address. Three base chains at priority filter - 10 run before the iptables policy, so a listed address is refused even if a policy rule would have allowed it. Inbound drops are silent to the outside; outbound blocks reject with administratively prohibited, so a local program fails at once instead of hanging. Each blocking rule logs with the prefix STOCKFW-TI list, rate-limited, and carries a counter. Verified test-intelset.sh Windows On Windows the rules are written and read in-process through the firewall’s COM interfaces (no netsh, no PowerShell), all in the group StockFW Threat Intel; the policy’s rules are named StockFW-… outside it, so neither apply nor clear touches the other. Each rule blocks one direction and one address family for at most a thousand ranges, and the whole set is replaced as one unit. Windows Firewall keeps no per-rule count, so the page says it cannot show how many connections each list blocked, and the Security log names no list, so an Events row on Windows shows the block without the list’s name. Known gap The store is %ProgramData%\\StockFW\\intel, readable by administrators only. There is no stockfw intel on Windows; the page is the whole tool there. The policy and the lists never touch each other. Applying, reloading or clearing the policy leaves the threat-intel table or rule group alone. The user-facing Stop firewall suspends the lists as well, and Start resumes them, because “stop the firewall” should mean what it says. On Linux the boot service brings the lists back with the policy; see the FAQ. What the kernel is really doing# The table, or the rule group, carries a digest of the rule set that produced it. Every surface compares what is live with what StockFW last applied and shows one of these states, never just the configuration: StateMeaning offThe master switch is off, or no list is enabled. enforcedThe live table is the one StockFW last applied. not loadedSomething should be enforced but the table or rule group is missing: a reboot without the boot service, or someone ran nft flush ruleset. differsA table or rule group is there, but not the one StockFW applied. suspendedYou stopped the firewall, so the lists are off on purpose. unsupported, needs rights, unknownCannot enforce here (nft missing), cannot read the engine without root or administrator rights, cannot tell. Linux Counters survive updates: before a set is replaced, i"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","u":"docs/troubleshooting.html","s":"Reference","h":[["Privileges","privileges"],["Events","events"],["Policy and Apply","policy-and-apply"],["IPv6","ipv6"],["Copilot and Sentinel","copilot-and-sentinel"],["Tray and window","tray-and-window"],["Command line and dashboard","command-line-and-dashboard"],["Uninstalling and starting over","uninstalling-and-starting-over"],["Reporting a problem","reporting-a-problem"]],"x":"Troubleshooting StockFW tries to say what is wrong in the place where it is wrong. This page collects those messages, says what each one means, and what fixes it. Privileges# “Administrator privileges required”, “Not live yet: run `sudo stockfw apply’”, exit status 3 You are changing the firewall without the rights to do it. The change is saved and will be applied the next time Apply runs with rights. On Windows use the StockFW (Elevated) shortcut; on Linux run sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW or sudo stockfw apply. Nothing was half-applied. “sudo: StockFW: command not found” Linux sudo does not search /usr/local/bin on Fedora, RHEL and some Kali systems (Ubuntu’s default includes it). Type the full path: sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW. The installer warns about this and deliberately does not edit sudoers; see why. “libcrypto.so.3 is OpenSSL 3” from install.sh Linux The distribution ships only OpenSSL 1.1 (Debian 11, Ubuntu 20.04). StockFW links OpenSSL 3 and liblzma 5 to read the signed location database, and the installer stops before copying anything rather than install a program and a boot service that could not start. Upgrade the distribution, or build from source against the libraries it has. “nft not found” or “curl not found” from install.sh Linux A warning, not a failure. Without nft the threat-intel lists and country rules cannot be loaded, and without curl they cannot be downloaded; the Threat Intel page says so. sudo apt install nftables curl. “Windows protected your PC” when running the installer The installer is not code-signed yet. Check the SHA-256 against the download page, then More info, Run anyway. Events# “Monitoring is not available” on Windows The audit policy that makes Windows write firewall events is off. Open the elevated app once; it enables it. Or run auditpol /set /subcategory:\"Filtering Platform Connection\" /success:enable /failure:enable in an administrator PowerShell. See Enable monitoring. If a domain policy forces auditing off, StockFW cannot override it and says so. “Monitoring is not available” on Linux The kernel log needs root. Start the app with sudo, or add yourself to the adm group for the command line’s events. The list stays empty although the firewall is running Read the sentence under the empty list. “Waiting for the first event” means nothing has been blocked or logged yet; try an inbound connection from another machine to a closed port. On Linux, allowed connections are logged only with Log allowed connections on; otherwise the page shows drops and rejects only. No events in WSL2, a container or a network namespace Linux The kernel delivers firewall log lines from a non-initial network namespace only when net.netfilter.nf_log_all_netns=1. Set it on the host: sudo sysctl -w net.netfilter.nf_log_all_netns=1. WSL2’s kernel also needs the netfilter log modules; a stock Ubuntu WSL image has them. “(up to N skipped)” next to the event count Windows More than 10,000 Security-log records arrived in one five-second poll. The newest 10,000 were read and the rest counted as an upper bound; the Security log keeps them all. On Linux the kernel log is read as it streams and nothing is skipped. “Another copy of StockFW is saving the history” Two copies of StockFW are running on the same account’s history folder, and only one may write it. The second lists events but saves nothing, and takes over by itself once the first is gone. StockFW --quit asks the running copy to quit. The Program column is empty Linux The kernel log carries no program name. The Overview’s connection table names programs for connections that are open; the Events page cannot for ones that are not. Known gap Policy and Apply# A rule I added is not applied Linux Check which policy you edited. As yourself the policy is ~/.stockfw; as root it is /etc/stockfw. stockfw status prints the directory in use. Pick one identity for policy work; see Two homes. “N malformed policy rules were skipped” A line in a policy file did not parse: a non-numeric port, a host name starting with a hyphen, a stray comma. The line was skipped, not guessed at. Open the file named in the message, fix or delete the line, reload. Saving from the app writes only the rules that parsed. The format. Apply says “policy held, firewall untouched” Audit-only on the Policy page or Lock policy on the Overview is on. The change was measured and recorded; the engine was left alone. Turn the hold off and Apply again. Apply failed and “the last good policy was restored” The engine refused a rule. The dialog carries the tool’s own message; on Windows it is often a target netsh cannot parse, on Linux a rule the kernel lacks a module for. The policy on the wire is what it was before you pressed Apply. The audit log has the line. “The forward to 192.168.1.30 was left out: no external interface is set” Port forwards need router mode: set the Internet connected network device and Enable Internet connection sharing on the Network Settings preference page. “NOT BLOCKED: name could not be looked up” A deny rule’s target is a host name that did not resolve while the firewall was being started, so it was left out rather than refuse to start at all. Fix the name or use the address, then Apply again; while the firewall is running, the same failure refuses the apply and keeps the old addresses blocked instead. A host-name rule covers nothing on Windows Windows Windows Firewall takes addresses only. Use the address, or one rule per address. The Covers column warns. IPv6# The IPv6 tile reads “Not filtered: ip6tables is not installed” Linux sudo apt install iptables brings ip6tables on Debian-family systems; dnf install iptables-nft on Fedora. Then Apply again. Until then only IPv4 is filtered, and the app says so rather than pretending. Known gap “None on this system” The kernel has no IPv6 stack. Nothing to filter, nothing wrong. Copilot and Sentinel# Sentinel blocked something I use Press Undo on it under Changes made by the Copilot, or right-click the destination on the Events page and Allow destination: your Allow lifts the block and the two reach the firewall together. The block would have expired by itself in ten minutes; it cannot come back in this session. If it happens often, set Sentinel to Detect only, which takes every staged block down at once. Sentinel never blocks loopback, your LAN, multicast or a destination the program has used before; if it did, that is a bug worth reporting with the audit lines. No findings appear The page says why: no events yet, monitoring off (with the reason the Events page gives), or “No findings from N events”, which means none of the fixed checks matched, not that the computer is safe. Allowed traffic is seen only when allowed-event logging is on; without it the login check sees only refused attempts. A slow pattern can stay below every limit; the limits are on the Copilot page. A health finding says “no fix offered” The fix was tried against the policy with the What-If engine and one of its checks failed, for example a narrowing that would have cut off a second private network this computer is on. The failed check is printed under the finding. Nothing was changed. Tray and window# No tray icon Your desktop has no tray host (GNOME without the AppIndicator extension, or a headless session). The window works as usual and closing it quits. See Background and the tray. No tray icon on Wayland or in WSL, and both tray switches are greyed out Linux GTK has a tray only on X11 and Windows. On a Wayland session (GNOME and KDE Plasma by default, and WSL’s WSLg) StockFW makes no icon: a background start shows the window, closing the window quits, and Preferences greys Show a tray icon and Close and minimize to the tray with the reason. Under sudo StockFW runs on XWayland instead, where the tray works as on X11. No Gtk-CRITICAL line is printed at start. Verified test-notray.sh “This application can not open files” after StockFW dashboard or StockFW status Linux That was the 1.0.7 me"},{"t":"Trust","u":"docs/trust.html","s":"Using the app","h":[["Inspect a file","inspect-a-file"],["Scan running processes","scan-running-processes"],["Attest and forget","attest-and-forget"],["The baseline file","the-baseline-file"],["What it is for","what-it-is-for"]],"x":"Trust A firewall decides by address and port, but the question behind most of its events is “do I trust the program that made this connection?”. The Trust page answers that for one file at a time: who signed it, what its hash is, and whether it is still the file you approved. Three attested programs, all in sync with their baseline. On Linux there is no code signature to read, so Publisher says “no signer” rather than guessing. Inspect a file# Type or paste a path and press Inspect. The report shows the file, its publisher, its signature state, the SHA-256 of its bytes, size, modification time, and how it compares with the baseline. Hashing a large executable takes a moment and runs off the main thread; the page says “Hashing and checking the signature…” and refuses a second task until the first finishes, so two reports can never be mixed up. Verified ui-trustview-smoke Signature lineMeaning Signed and verifiedAn Authenticode signature whose digest matches the file and whose chain stands. Signed but invalidThere is a signature, but it does not hold: the file was edited after signing, the certificate expired or is distrusted. This is the interesting negative. Not signedA Windows executable with no signature. Not an executableNo MZ header. Hashing it still makes sense; signing does not apply. Signature not checkedNo signature provider on this platform. This is what Linux shows, honestly, instead of “unsigned”. Windows The check uses WinVerifyTrust and CryptQueryObject with revocation lookups turned off, so it never touches the network and never hangs on a machine without one. The publisher is the signer’s display name. Verified test_trust_engine Linux There is no platform signature to read, so the page offers the hash, the file facts and the baseline. Package-manager signatures (dpkg, rpm) are not consulted. Known gap Scan running processes# Scan processes lists the executable behind every process this account can see and assesses each against the baseline, so a new or changed program shows up without you knowing its path. On Linux the paths come from /proc/<pid>/exe; processes owned by other users are readable only as root, and the status line says so when the list is empty for that reason. Attest and forget# Attest records the inspected file, its hash, its publisher and the time in the baseline, after a dialog that shows the path and the hash and says that the attestation goes to the audit log. From then on the file’s row reads one of: in sync: the bytes on disk match the attested hash; CHANGED: same path, different bytes. The report says which hash the baseline remembers. An update you installed produces this too, which is the point: you look, and attest again if it was you; missing: attested earlier, now gone; unreadable: the file could not be read at all; new: never attested. Forget removes a path from the baseline, with the same kind of confirmation. Refresh list reassesses every row. Both attest and forget append a line to audit.log (trust-attest, trust-forget). If the baseline cannot be written, the page says “Attested in memory, but not saved to disk” rather than pretending. The baseline file# trust.csv in your profile folder, one quoted row per path: path, SHA-256, size, modification time, signature state, publisher, first seen, last seen. Paths compare without regard to letter case, because Windows paths do. A row that does not parse is skipped, never the whole file; a missing file is an empty baseline. STOCKFW_TRUST_FILE points the app and the tests at another file. See Files and formats. Verified test_trust_engine What it is for# Trust does not change the firewall. It gives you a second kind of evidence next to the Events page: when the Copilot reports a program doing something new, Inspect tells you whether the program itself is still what it was. The two together are how you tell an update from a replacement."},{"t":"Changelog","u":"changelog.html","s":"Changelog","h":[["{{version}} · DATE · current release","v1-0-8"],["Added","added"],["Changed","changed"],["Fixed","fixed"],["1.0.7 · 3 November 2026","v1-0-7"],["Added","added-2"],["Changed","changed-2"],["Fixed","fixed-2"],["1.0.6 · development version, shipped as part of 1.0.7","v1-0-6"],["1.0.5 · 11 October 2026 · the hardening release","v1-0-5"],["1.0.4 · October 2026","v1-0-4"]],"x":"Each release also adds a chapter to the architecture and security document, which records the evidence behind every claim below. This page is the short version. 1.0.8 · DATE · current release# The terminal release. On Linux, stockfw now does from a terminal what the window does, and says what is true: whether the saved policy is live, who can reach each open port, what the audit log holds, and what Sentinel has staged. Every sample on the command line page is this release’s real output. Added# The live event line. stockfw events -f prints new events in the table’s columns and, on a terminal, keeps a status line under them: firewall state, live or pending, HELD (audit-only), blocked today, Sentinel and the event source. Never shown with --json, --csv, -q or in a pipe, so machine output stays one event per line. When the reader stops, -f says why and exits 1. The live event line. Verified test-cli.sh Events triage. events --summary counts a window per source and per port with a bar per hour (--by, --top); --wide, --csv, -r, --utc; --port takes a list and --dir takes both. The RULE column names the threat-intel list that blocked an event; 3389 is Remote Desktop and 161 is SNMP, in the terminal and on the Events page. Verified test-cli.sh stockfw status says whether what you saved is live: “12 rules, live” or “1 change not applied”, the last apply, start or stop, the packets blocked today and the last one, and whether stockfw.service puts the rules back at boot. -v adds the firewall settings, the event history and Sentinel; -q prints one line. Monitoring is decided by what your account can read, tried rather than guessed from your user id. Verified test-cli.sh New commands. stockfw audit reads the audit log with filters and -f; stockfw sentinel list shows Sentinel’s staged blocks and when each lifts; stockfw listening shows every open port, the program behind it and whether the policy lets the LAN and the internet reach it, with the rule that decides; stockfw check lists the lines of the policy files that are not rules. audit, sentinel, listening, check. Verified test-cli.sh Rules have references. inbound-allow-service:2 works in policy show, remove, list --status, simulate --json and copilot health --json; add and remove echo the reference and check the rule’s own traffic. policy diff shows the saved policy against the applied one, and apply --dry-run lists the rows it would add and remove. Verified test-cli.sh The lockout guard. Over SSH, apply and start check that the new policy still lets your session in, under sudo too, with sshd on any port; they ask on a terminal and refuse without one unless --yes is given. SSH clients on every port sshd listens on are in the set a blocklist may never cut. The lockout guard. Verified test-cli.sh Verified test-hostnet.c Global options and honest tables. --plain, --full and --color=auto|always|never; tables fit the terminal and only the terminal; symbols fall back to ASCII; a hint that needs root names the program by its full path. Text from logs and comments is escaped before it reaches the terminal, everywhere. -q simulate prints only the verdict word; copilot health --severity sets the level that gives exit 7; simulate --applied asks the applied policy. Verified test-cli.sh connections and interfaces, in detail. Both ports and the TCP state, filters by address, port, program, direction and state, counts with --by, connected UDP with --udp; rates measured over the real time between samples, --watch=SECS, -c COUNT, --all, one line per frame with --json. Verified test-sockets.c man stockfw, and completion for bash and zsh. The manual page is written by the tool from the same tables its parser reads, so it cannot fall behind; completion offers commands, options, values and your own rule references. The Linux installer puts all three under /usr/local/share. Manual page and completion. Verified test-cli.sh The dashboard, hardened. It serves your own account only: a connection from root or another user is closed unread, and as plain root it refuses to start. The link holds a code that works once, for two minutes, printed only to a terminal (a pipe gets a private launch file); the page swaps it for a session that reads for 12 hours; stockfw dashboard link makes a new link; a link used twice ends every session and the terminal says so; a fingerprint in the terminal and on the page lets you check they match. Only http://127.0.0.1:PORT is accepted, not localhost. Request deadlines and per-account slots mean slow senders cannot lock you out; the one POST needs the page’s own Origin; Trusted Types and two more headers. --origin-port N for ssh -L; under WSL a Windows-side browser is refused unless --allow-windows. Closing the terminal stops it cleanly. Still read-only, still 127.0.0.1 only. Dashboard. Verified test-dashboard.sh A build of the command line alone. cmake -DFS_BUILD_GUI=OFF builds stockfw and its manual page without GTK. CI checks that stockfw needs no GTK, GDK, Pango or cairo library. Verified test-nogui-build.sh Changed# Options are read the way you meant them. The word after an option that takes a value is that value; everything after -- is a plain word; an unknown option is refused (exit 2) instead of ignored or folded into a simulate question; a typo gets “did you mean…?”. apply after stop starts the firewall again. A stopped or unreadable firewall gets the whole policy and the audit line says why. A core fix, so the desktop app’s Apply gets it too. Lines that are not rules are named, with their file, line and reason. apply refuses them, and so do policy add and remove, which would have deleted such a line without a word. SSH,,Anyone still loads. start and stop say “Already running” or “Already stopped” and write no audit line of their own; a stop on a stopped firewall still takes the blocklists down, logged as intel-suspend. --since and --until take an ISO time without seconds. stockfw version prints one line. The Linux README and the installer no longer say that every Debian and Kali sudo leaves out /usr/local/bin; Ubuntu’s default includes it, Fedora, RHEL and some Kali systems leave it out. Fixed# Wayland desktops and WSL: no tray icon, no start-up warning. GTK has a tray only on X11 and Windows; StockFW no longer tries to make one on Wayland (GNOME, KDE Plasma, WSLg), so the Gtk-CRITICAL line at every start is gone, a background start shows the window, and Preferences greys both tray switches with the reason. Background, tray and sign-in. Verified test-notray.sh The window keeps up with a firewall stopped from outside, with or without a tray icon: the once-a-minute re-read of the firewall state ran only while a tray icon existed. StockFW dashboard says what to run instead of GTK’s “This application can not open files”, and exits with status 2. install.sh reads sudo’s secure_path in the unquoted form RHEL and Fedora write, so the warning appears there too. events refuses --blocked with --allowed and an unknown --proto (exit 2) instead of letting the last flag win; the first interfaces --watch frame showed half the rate. 1.0.7 · 3 November 2026# Added# The What-If engine. A parser that reads a question the way you say it and shows how it read it; an engine that models your platform’s firewall (first match on Linux, block wins on Windows) together with the Firewall preferences, Sentinel’s blocks, whether the firewall runs and whether the saved policy is live; DEPENDS answers that spell out the split; the whole trace of rules checked; and fixes verified against the policy before they are offered. A window beside the page, not over it, and the same answer from stockfw simulate, with exit 6 for DEPENDS. Verified test-whatif.c Verified ui-simviz-smoke Preferences redesigned. A grouped sidebar, a title and purpose line on every page, settings in titled groups, change marks before you save, and firewall settings applied under the last applied policy. Preferences. Verified ui-prefs-smoke Background mode and the"},{"t":"Download","u":"download.html","s":"Download","h":[["StockFW {{release}}","release"],["Check the download","verify"],["Requirements","requirements"],["Build from source","build"],["Build on Windows (MSYS2)","build-windows"],["Build on Linux","build-linux"],["Uninstall","uninstall"],["Older versions","older"]],"x":"StockFW 1.0.8# Both downloads come from the project’s GitHub Releases page; this site does not host files. The size and SHA-256 of each file are listed on the release page next to it. PlatformFileNotes Windows 10 and 11, 64-bitStockFW-1.0.8-Setup.exeNSIS installer, about 45 MB. Bundles the GTK3 runtime, so nothing else needs installing, and ships the README, the changelog and the licence texts. Linux x86-64StockFW-1.0.8-linux-x86_64.tar.gzTarball with install.sh, about 1.2 MB. Needs GTK 3, liblzma, OpenSSL 3, iptables and ip6tables from your distribution; installs a boot service that puts the rules back after a restart. You can try it before installing: unpacked, ./StockFW opens the app and sudo ./cli/stockfw dashboard serves the dashboard. ChecksumsSHA256SUMS.txtSHA-256 of both files. Not code-signed The Windows installer is not code-signed, so SmartScreen shows “Windows protected your PC”. Choose More info, then Run anyway, after you have checked the checksum below. The checksum is your verification. Check the download# Compare the output with the matching line in SHA256SUMS.txt. PowerShell (Windows)CopyPS> Get-FileHash .\\StockFW-1.0.8-Setup.exe -Algorithm SHA256 Terminal (Linux)Copy$ sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt --ignore-missing StockFW-1.0.8-linux-x86_64.tar.gz: OK Requirements# WindowsWindows 10 or 11, 64-bit. Windows Defender Firewall must be present (it always is). To change rules, run the StockFW (Elevated) shortcut; everything else works without administrator rights. LinuxAn x86-64 distribution with GTK 3 (libgtk-3-0), liblzma (liblzma5) and OpenSSL 3 (libssl3, which provides libcrypto.so.3; Debian 11 and Ubuntu 20.04 ship only OpenSSL 1.1 and are refused by the installer), iptables and ip6tables; nft and curl for threat-intel blocklists and country blocking. Tested on Debian, Kali, Ubuntu and WSL2. Reading events and applying rules need root; sudo /usr/local/bin/StockFW is the full tool. Step-by-step instructions: Install on Windows, Install on Linux. Build from source# The release is built from the tag v1.0.8 in the repository. The same code builds on either platform in a few commands. Build on Windows (MSYS2)# Install MSYS2, open the UCRT64 shell (as administrator if you want to apply rules from the build), then: MSYS2 UCRT64 shellCopy$ pacman -S --needed base-devel git mingw-w64-ucrt-x86_64-{toolchain,gtk3,pkg-config,ninja,cmake,xz,openssl} $ git clone --branch v1.0.8 https://github.com/Cazo-Net/StockFW.git $ cd StockFW/port $ cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja $ cmake --build build $ ./build/StockFW.exe To make the installer from the build, run installer/build_installer.sh; it stages the GTK runtime beside the executable and fails if any launcher still carries old branding. Build on Linux# Debian, Kali, Ubuntu, WSL2Copy$ sudo apt install build-essential cmake ninja-build pkg-config git libgtk-3-dev liblzma-dev libssl-dev iptables nftables curl $ git clone --branch v1.0.8 https://github.com/Cazo-Net/StockFW.git $ cd StockFW/port $ cmake -S . -B build-linux -G Ninja $ cmake --build build-linux $ sudo ./build-linux/StockFW # the desktop app $ ./build-linux/stockfw status # the command line To make the same tarball the release ships, run tools/build-linux.sh (a stripped Release build) and then installer/linux/build_installer_linux.sh. tests/test-release.sh checks the result against the version in CMakeLists.txt, the changelog section and the licence list, as CI does for every release. Uninstall# WindowsSettings → Apps, or the Uninstall shortcut in the StockFW Start menu folder. Firewall rules StockFW created stay in Windows Firewall; remove them with Stop firewall in the app first, or in wf.msc (they are all named StockFW-…). Linuxsudo /usr/local/bin/stockfw-uninstall removes the binaries, the menu entry and the icons. Your policy in ~/.stockfw or /etc/stockfw is kept; delete it yourself if you want it gone. Older versions# Every tagged release, with its notes, is on the GitHub Releases page. The changelog describes what changed in each."}];
